content:
\begin{title}
A Statistical-Thermodynamic Modeling of Ordering Phenomena in Binary Intermetallic B2- and L12-Structures**
\end{title}

By Olga Semenova* and Regina Krachler

Statistical-thermodynamic models for the description of ordering in non-stoichiometric intermetallic phases can be very helpful for the design of new functional materials for various purposes. Here, we present a review of methods for describing B2 (BCC) and L12 (FCC) – type binary alloys. The free energy of binary alloys where ordered structures are formed, is evaluated by statistical approaches employing applications of the Bragg–Williams–Gorsky mean-ﬁeld approximation. In the original mean ﬁeld method, the degree of order in the neighborhood of a given site in the crystal is determined by the average state of order throughout the macroscopic crystal. Actually, the force tending to produce long-range order depends on the ﬂuctuations in the conﬁguration, an effect which is neglected in the mean ﬁeld approximation but is taken under consideration, e.g., in the Quasi-Chemical Approach (QCA) by Guggenheim and its advanced applications, in various sophisticated treatments using the Cluster Variation Method (CVM) originally developed by Kikuchi, as well as in the Defect Correlation Model (DCM). The latter model is a less sophisticated method based on the simultaneous description of short-range order and long-range order by use of properly deﬁned non-overlapping clusters. All these methods allow the ‘‘pure’’ mean-ﬁeld results to be substantially reﬁned, particularly at high temperatures, and for alloys with large deviations from the stoichiometric composition. Defect concentration-alloy composition diagrams and activity-alloy compo- sition diagrams are shown for several methods with focus on the QCA and DCM approaches. The calculated results are compared with each other and with experimental data on thermodynamic and structural properties from the literature.

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

The role of models in materials sciences has a paramount signiﬁcance since the turn of the 20th century. Historically,

\begin{title}
[*] Dr. O. Semenova
\end{title}

[*] Dr. O. Semenova Department of Inorganic Chemistry – Materials Chemistry, University of Vienna, Waehringerstr. 42, A-1090 Wien, Austria E-mail: olga.semenova@univie.ac.at Prof. R. Krachler Department of Inorganic Chemistry, University of Vienna, Waehringerstr. 42, A-1090 Wien, Austria

[**] Financial support of this study by the Austrian Science Founda- tion (under projects no. P20463-N19) is gratefully acknowledged. In dedication of the 65 anniversary of Prof. Herbert Ipser.

modeling is a central feature in the growth philosophy of the knowledge of natural science and, particularly, in the recent years. The importance of theoretical models for an under- standing of the structure–composition–processing–property relationships in non-stoichiometric intermetallic compounds is well-recognized in materials research and technology, since these phases are a fascinating group of materials, both from the point of view of their fundamental properties and their practical and commercial use in high-performance strategic applications. The physico-chemical and mechanical proper- ties of metals and their alloys are closely related to their structural properties and, particularly, to the processes which proceed in their structure under different treatment condi- tions. To control the processes of crystallization and growth of new phase, recrystallization and recovery, creep, fatigue, rupture, wetting, magnetization, resistivity, and many others

a profound basic knowledge of order–disorder processes and processes of creation of point defects in the structure of materials is required.

Ordering processes in intermetallic systems belong to those few phase transition phenomena which, within the framework of certain assumptions, can in principle be described completely by using statistical-thermodynamic methods.[1]

\begin{title}
2. The Mean-Field Theory and Its Refinement
\end{title}

The ﬁrst description of order–disorder phase transitions was the mean-ﬁeld theory published in 1928 by Gorski,[2] and, independently, in 1934 by Bragg and Williams.[3] The main concept of the theory is based on the long-range order. This method leads to a theory predicting a critical temperature, sometimes with and sometimes without a latent heat.

Actually the ordering energy between any pair of atoms depends on the arrangement of their immediate neighbors, and only indirectly on the mean distribution of atoms upon the sites of the entire lattice. The ﬁrst step which consists of accounting for these short-range effects was taken by Bethe and Willis.[4] Later on, the Bethe method was reﬁned with account for four-body correlation.[5,6]

Thequasi-chemicalapproach(QCA)ofBethe–Guggenheim,[4,7] as well as the earlier Bragg–Williams–Gorsky[2,3,8] approximation are the two ﬁrst approximations in a hierarchy of the Cluster Variation Method (CVM) developed in the 1950s by Kikuchi.[9] The CVM itself or its variations like the Probability Path (PP) method – an extension of CVM upon kinetics[10] – is the basis of the analytical conﬁgurational thermodynamics of solid compounds.[9,11–15]

By now many procedures exist which allow accounting for the long-range ordering (LRO) or short-range ordering (SRO) in free energy calculations, with this or that degree of accuracy. In detail they are reviewed by de Fontaine in ref.[14,15] Very successfully and frequently used, are the CVM[9] along with the quasi-chemical (QC) method of Guggenheim[7] and the Bragg–Williams approximation,[2,3,8,14,15] the Taylor and Fourier expansions[16] or Landau-Lifshitz theory,[17] as well as the method of Static Concentration Waves (SCW),[9,14,15,18] or the Regular Solution Model.[19,20]

At the same time, various types of ‘‘pure’’ mean-ﬁeld models were developed in order to understand the appear- ance of non-stoichiometry in intermetallic compounds, and the relationship between LRO, energies of formation of defects, and thermodynamic properties.[21–40] Mostly they are based on the types of defects present in the lattice and on evaluations of disorder parameters which are usually deﬁned as the concentrations of point defects at the stoichiometric composition. By deﬁnition, the ‘‘pure’’ mean-ﬁeld models neglect any effects of SRO in the crystal lattice which causes an inherent error in the temperature range close to the order–disorder transformation where long-range order (LRO) clearly breaks down but short-range order (SRO) may still play an important role.

A signiﬁcant number of studies have been performed to characterize the point defects and thermodynamic activity in binary compounds with the B2- and L12-crystal structure. Many of these studies have been summarized in the reviews by Chang and Neumann[36] and Pike et al.,[41] with some additional studies being done since that time.[42–49]

\begin{title}
3. Advanced Approaches
\end{title}

More sophisticated approaches for modeling phase equili- bria and ordering phenomena in alloys are based on diverse ab initio calculations. These methods have been in detail reviewed by Ruban and Abrikosov.[50] It is obvious that the quantum-mechanical approaches are correct and successful. However, there are many technical constraints which limit their application. The very common constraint concerns temperature. In this area for the quantitative modeling of temperature-dependent regime, the method for calculating effective cluster interactions in metallic alloys, the Cluster Expansion (CE) Approach, is the most advanced and rigorous.[15] Up-to-date CEs are done in combination with Density Functional Theory (DFT) methods and Monte–Carlo (MC) simulations thus providing temperature dependent phase stabilities with DFT precision.

An ample of papers with Monte–Carlo or Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulations of chemical ordering in inter- metallics has been published in the past decades. An example of MC works which are dealing with ordering kinetics is the papers of Oramus et al. [51] dedicated to L12-ordered Ni3Al, and Biborski et al. [52] dedicated to the B2-ordered ‘‘triple- defect’’ NiAl. The MC simulations yield exact solutions of the corresponding Ising models.[53]

Going beyond the Ising models,[53] a good example of modeling of the defect structure and order–disorder transi- tions is the MC simulations based on the Analytical Bond-Order Potentials (ABOP). This formalism is very successful employed for modeling transition metals with a variety of crystal structures.[54]

The effect of system ﬂuctuations on the stability of alloy phases has been addressed by Khachaturyan.[18] In this theory, the loss of stability with respect to SCW characterized by ﬁnite wave vectors leads to the order–disorder transforma- tion. The theory of SCW can be employed to predict phase structure, stability, critical temperature, and kinetics.

Many works are basing on the ﬁrst-principles or Embedded Atom Method calculations (EAM) in conjunction with statistical mechanics[55–62] for the theoretical evaluation of point defect concentrations in binary intermetallics. These studies greatly extended the knowledge of the properties of intermetallic compounds related to point defects. The EAM model may be classiﬁed as a semi- empirical one and is based on the point defect ‘‘gas’’ model, i.e., the interactions between the point defects are neglected. The effective thermodynamic computation (e.g., MC or MD simulations) involving directly CE or other DFT energetics is still technically limited to rather simple

cases. Therefore, the majority of atomistic modeling of complex systems is performed with EAM.

\begin{title}
4. Approaches for Intermetallic Compounds with L12-Structure
\end{title}

Many intermetallic A3B-compounds crystallize in the cubic L12-structure. In fact, L12-intermetallics attract a considerable attention due to their unique chemical, mechanical, and physical properties like high strength, stiffness, and good oxidation and environmental resis- tance.[63] An extensive research in the past two decades has been aiming at an improvement of their mechanical, electrical, corrosive, and other speciﬁc properties. Many of these beneﬁcial properties are closely related to the crystal structure or the degree of LRO, i.e., to the amount of point defects in the lattice, which can be vacancies, anti-structure atoms, and interstitial defects. They are present in thermo- dynamic equilibrium and are also responsible for the deviation from stoichiometry.

First a statistical thermodynamic model for L12-phases, and particularly for Ni3Al, was developed by Foiles and Daw.[64] The EAM was applied for calculations of various point defect properties including vacancy properties. A good agreement with experimental data of Wang et al. [65] was found for the formation and migration energy of the Ni-vacancies. The crucial feature of Ni3Al is that the Ni-vacancy formation energy is much lower than that for Al-vacancy formation. This is resulting from all known models – vacancies reside almost exclusively on Ni-sublattice. Later on several attempts were performed to calculate defect formation energies.[56,66–69]

Another statistical-thermodynamic model for L12- compounds was developed in ref.[70] based on a mean-ﬁeld approximation for non-interactive point defects. The expres- sions for the defect concentration as a function of composition and temperature were derived. This model was applied to the intermetallic compound Ni3Al using defect formation energies from the literature[70,71] and compared to experi- mental data of Steiner et al.[72]

\begin{title}
5. Descriptions of Point Defect Formation in B2-Phases
\end{title}

Numerous intermetallic compounds crystallize in the cubic B2-structure. These compounds have outstanding properties as high-temperature structural materials of the future. The advantageous mechanical properties of these materials are closely linked to the existence of LRO in the structure. Often B2-phases exist over wide homogeneity range and non-stoichiometry is based on one important feature of the B2-structure: its ability to form two sorts of point defects on both sublattices, vacancies, and anti-structure atoms.

There were only a few models which aimed at a comprehensive explanation of different defect types in B2-intermetallics. Earlier studies considered the vacancy

formation as completely different and treated the different B2-phases by different models. The ﬁrst attempt to calculate vacancy concentration in ordered alloys was undertaken by Cheng et al.[22] This energy-vacancy model developed for B2-phases does not take into account local rearrangements of atoms around a vacancy after its creation although the formation free energy for a vacancy on a given site in alloy is mainly dependent on its local atomic environment. Applying this model to CuZn it was found that above the critical ordering temperature Tc, the vacancy concentration on a- and b-sites are indistinguishable, but below Tc, it becomes different, with fewer vacancies on a-sites. The average vacancy formation energy increases with LRO parameter.

Another model of Chang et al.[23] which use an approach proposed by Neumann et al.,[24] tries to deduce vacancy properties from thermodynamic activities, which have been experimentally available.[25,26] Over a wide temperature range, where the thermodynamic properties have been measured, their model ﬁts well the temperature and the concentration dependence of the component activities in B2-phases.

The model for point defect formation in B2-intermetallics proposed by Ren and Otsuka,[27] accounts for all kinds of defect structures including triple-defect type, anti-structure defect type, and hybrid type and the temperature inﬂuence on the point defect concentration. This model is based on the statement that point defect formation in intermetallics is different from that in single-lattice crystals and controlled by the competition between the two entropy production processes: vacancy formation or anti-structure defect forma- tion in the two sublattices. The calculated composition and temperature dependencies of the vacancy concentration were in good agreement with experimental data for all three different types of B2-intermetallics, i.e., near triple-defect type, hybrid type, and near anti-structure type.

Other theoretical calculations of defect concentrations in non-stoichiometric B2 compounds have been done, e.g., by Stolen and Gronvold[28] and Libowitz and Lightstone.[29] The latter authors used a mean-ﬁeld approximation on the basis of the Ising model.[53] These models are based on the statisti- cal-thermodynamic formalism of the canonical ensemble, which is adapted to the treatment of equilibria in closed systems. Therefore, this approach was criticized by Bakker and van Ommen.[30]

Considerable input in development of statistical-thermo- dynamic models for intermetallic phases was made in ref.[32,38,39,70,73–77] These theoretical models are based on the approach of Wagner–Schottky[78] for non-interacting point defects where the introduction of vacancies into the system implies the transfer of particles to or from the outside. Later on in ref.[79,80] a Defect Correlation Model (DCM) for description of the arrangement of anti-structure and vacancy defects in B2-phases was developed, a method based on the simultaneous description of SRO and LRO by use of non-overlapping clusters.

\begin{title}
6. Application of the Wagner--Schottky Mean-Field Approach
\end{title}

In this part, we show some of the principles which were used for the modeling of a defect structure in intermetallics with B2-, and L12-structures[39,70,71,76–78,81–88] applying a Wagner–Schottky Approach.[81] The Wagner–Schottky Approximation works under the assumption that (at constant pressure) the internal energy, volume, and vibrational entropy of the crystal are linear functions of the number of atoms or vacancies, respectively, in the different sublattices.[39] It is essential to note that this approach considers an ideal crystal lattice with non-interacting (i.e., isolated) atoms and point defects.

Many authors have presented statistical-thermodynamic models and derived expressions for the composition depen- dence of the thermodynamic properties in ordered inter- metallic phases using the Wagner–Schottky approach.[29–39] Most of these models are based on evaluations of disorder parameters which were usually deﬁned as the concentrations of point defects at the stoichiometric composition. Such models were also developed for B2-, and L12-phases[31–36,38,39,56,64–68,71,73] assuming purely substitutional disorder caused by anti- structure atoms.

Binary intermetallic compounds can be simply deﬁned as ordered alloy phases formed between two metallic elements A and B.[89] The high-temperature properties of such com- pounds are due to the formation of the long-range ordered superlattice which reduces dislocation mobility and diffusion processes at elevated temperatures.[90–92] Even though two or more materials may share the same crystal structure they may have different properties. The properties of a material depend on the type of atoms present, the way the atoms are held together (bonding), the crystal structure, and the defects present.

Let us consider a crystal lattice of a fully ordered stoichiometric compound AxBy, which is divided into two different sublattices labeled as a and b, and occupied properly by the two sorts of atoms A and B. The numbers of the sublattice sites Na and Nb are

\begin{token}
(1)
\end{token}

\begin{equation}
N^{\alpha}=x N^{g}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
N^{\beta}=y N^{g}
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
(2)
\end{token}

where Ng is the total number of lattice sites. Four types of point defects are allowed in the lattice. These are anti-structure atoms and vacancies on both sublattices: Na B, Nb A – number of B- or A-atoms on the a- or b-sublattice, respectively; Na V, Nb V – number of vacancies on the a- or b-sublattice, respectively.

To discover the interrelationship between lattice disorder and thermodynamic properties, consider AB-crystal as an open system which is allowed to exchange both energy and matter with its surroundings. Therefore, we use the grand canonical partition function J which exclusively contains the conﬁguration statistics of the lattice positions can be expressed

in the following form:[32]

\begin{equation}
\Xi=\sum_{n}\mathbf{e}^{\left[\mu_{A}\left(\left(N_{A}\right)_{n}/N_{L}\right)+\mu_{B}\left(\left(N_{B}\right)_{n}/N_{L}\right)-E_{n}\right]/k T}
\end{equation}

and correspondingly the grand canonical potential V:

\begin{title}
V ¼ kT ln J
\end{title}

In these equations, mA and mB are the chemical potentials of A and B, respectively; (NA)n and (NB)n are the numbers of A or B atoms, respectively, present inside the crystal when it is in the state n; En is the total conﬁgurational potential energy of the crystal when it is in the state n; NL is Avogadro’s number.

Introducing a degree of LRO h, and using a mean-ﬁeld approximation, we can deﬁne

\begin{equation}
\Omega(\eta)=-k T\ln\Xi_{\eta}
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
or
\end{token}

\begin{equation}
\Omega(\eta)=-k T\mathrm{l n}\sum_{i=1}^{W(\eta)}\mathrm{e}^{\left[\mu_{A}\left(N_{A}(\eta)/N_{L}\right)+\mu_{B}\left(N_{B}(\eta)/N_{L}\right)-U(\eta)\right]/k T}
\end{equation}

where W(h) is the probability term or the total number of all possible conﬁgurations with the same energy value Ei ¼ U(h).

By minimizing the grand canonical potential V(h), the corresponding equilibrium condition can be found:

\begin{equation}
\left(\cfrac{\partial\Omega(\eta)}{\partial\eta}\right)_{V,T,\mu_{A},\mu_{B}}=0.
\end{equation}

Finally, using the Gibbs–Duhem relationship

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{d}\mu_{A}=\mathrm{d}\mu_{B}\left(\frac{x_{B}}{x_{B}-1}\right)\quad\left(\mathrm{d}T=0,\mathrm{d}p=0\right)
\end{equation}

one can ﬁnd the expressions for equilibrium defect concen- trations Na B, Nb A, Na V, Nb V, mA, and mB.

The chemical potentials at ﬁnite temperatures T > 0, mi, were referred to their values mi,0 at the stoichiometric composition. The thermodynamic activities of the two components aA, and aB, as functions of composition, we can calculate with respect to the activities in the stoichiometric alloy aA,0 and aB,0:

\begin{equation}
\ln\frac{a_{i}}{a_{i,0}}=\frac{\mu_{i}-\mu_{i,0}}{RT}
\end{equation}

The modeling approach selected here uses the energies of formation of the different point defects as adjusted para- meters,[32,70,71,76,77,81–88] although, of course, some disorder or long range order parameter could be used equally well.[39] The advantage of using the energy parameters in the present case lies in the possibility to test the values that are available in the literature. In principle, all types of partial or integral thermodynamic properties can be calculated based on corresponding model equations. However, the compositional variation of the partial thermodynamic functions is most sensitive to changes in the degree of ordering in the crystal lattice, and the thermodynamic activity is the property that

\begin{token}
(3)
\end{token}

\begin{token}
(4)
