(9)
\end{token}

can be determined experimentally directly and, therefore, by the most accurate way.

This modeling approach was applied to compounds with B2-structure, e.g., NiAl in ref.,[32] and to several intermetallic compounds with L12-strucuture as Ni3Al,[70,71,83] Ni3Ga,[81–85,93] Pt3Ga,[83,86,87] and Pt3In in ref.[83,85,86]

The values of defect formation energy for L12-compounds listed in Table 1 have been taken as adjusted parameters for the model calculations. The output results on calculated thermodynamic activities were compared to experimental data, which can provide the possibility to decide which of the various values of the energies of defect formation reported in the literature may be consistent.

Numerous theoretical studies have investigated the energetics of defect formation in Ni3Al which differ by at least a factor of 2.[56,64,66–69] The variations in the values of the energies of formation for the possible point defects have consequences for their corresponding equilibrium concentra- tions. Among them the only one particular set of defect formation energies by Debiaggi et al.,[66] from computer simulation of many-body inter-atomic potentials, were chosen as parameters for the modeling. These data are in good agreement with the experimental activity curve of Steiner and Komarek.[72] Thermal disorder and the deviation from stoichiometry in the Ni3Al-phase seem to be caused entirely by anti-structure atoms.

Highly reliable defect formation energies are accessible from ab initio calculations based on the Generalized Gradient Approximation (GGA).[94] To prove the reliability of the Wagner–Schottky approach shown above (Eqs. 1 through 9), ab initio calculations were performed to provide the desired defect formation energies.[71] Vacancy and anti-site defect formation energies in L12-ordered Ni3Al were calculated using different ab initio approaches and different computa- tional methods.[71] Subsequently, the calculated defect for- mation energies were used as input parameters for the statistical-thermodynamic model. The comparison of the resulting activity curves to the available experimental data,[72,95] allowed an assessment of the quality of the statistical-thermodynamic model.

The presented modeling study was then extended to other intermetallic phases with the L12-structure as Ni3Ga, Pt3Ga,

