Two L12-compounds, Ni3Ga and Pt3In were chosen for derivation of the thermodynamic factor in diffusion from experimental data on thermodynamic activities of Ga and In in the temperature range between 1073 and 1273 K.[85] It was shown that the thermodynamic factor in diffusion in both compounds exhibits the maximum near the stoichiometric composition. This indicates a strong interaction between Ni and Ga atoms, as well as Pt and In atoms, as was expected for ordered alloys.

To summarize all these results it is important to note that this approach for modeling of ordering processes in several intermetallic compounds with L12-type structure demon- strated its reliability and robustness which was conﬁrmed by experimental results. Among the four compounds listed in Table 1, for Ni3Al six different sets of values of defect formation energies were available in the literature. One set[66] gave the best agreement to experimental data. As mentioned above, in the case of Ni3Ga, Pt3In, and Pt3Ga, defect formation energies were estimated using the modeling approach proposed in ref.[70] It was found that the energies of formation for anti-structure atoms are considerably higher for the two compounds containing platinum than for Ni3Al and Ni3Ga, resulting also in a much smaller disorder parameter a. This reﬂects much higher degree of order in the two platinum compounds which must be due to a noticeably tighter chemical bonding in the latter compounds.

\begin{title}
7. Application of DCM and Quasi-Chemical Models
\end{title}

A Generalized Statistical-Thermodynamic Model (a Generalized Defect Correlation Model, GDCM) was devel- oped for non-stoichiometric binary B2-compounds in ref.,[80] which is based on the Bethe–Bragg–Williams approxima- tion[3,4,8] and the well-known Ising Approach (pair interaction

approximation).[53] As input parameters for the model calculations were taken the pair interaction energies between atoms and defects. The GDCM is an extended version of the so-called DCM,[79,99] which is able to describe defect clusters formed by two, three, or even more atoms or vacancies in non-frustrated crystal lattices like the B2-structure. Thus DCM was adapted to these general cases, accounting for all four types of point defects in the B2-lattice and considering the inﬂuence of both, SRO and LRO. This approach can be applied to any kind of B2-phase, independently of the prevailing defect mechanism. Due to the possibility to account for defect correlations, the model is able to describe not only the behavior well below the order–disorder transformation (as previous models) but it is also able to describe the situation in the vicinity of the transition and above. The model includes also SRO in its equations and may be able to predict rather accurate order–disorder transitions. Introducing assumptions that restrict the complexity of the problem, the model can be extended to other ordered 1:1 compounds (as e.g., the L10-structure) or 1:3 compounds (L12-structure).

Recently,[100] an extension of the GDCM employing a QC argument for description of thermodynamic behavior and ordering phenomena in B2 ordered binary intermetallic materials has been proposed. Both modeling approaches, GDCM, as it was derived in ref.[80] and QCA,[100] have been applied for description of thermodynamic activities and vacancy concentrations over composition range and tempera- ture in non-stoichiometric B2-ordered intermetallic com- pounds with triple-defect mechanism (NiAl, FeAl, CoAl, PdAl, PdIn, NiGa, and CoGa), as well as with substitutional (anti-structure) defect mechanism (FeCo, NiZn, and AgMg).

Only in one case the pair-wise interaction energies partly were available in the literature (NiAl),[30,58,99,101] and these were used for the calculations. In other cases, no literature data on ab initio values of pair interaction energies were found, therefore, it was attempted to estimate these sets of interaction energy parameters from a comparison of the calculated composition dependence of the activities and/or point defect concentrations to experimental data. For both models, the same sets of pair-wise interaction energies have been used. The full sets of pair interaction energies suggested

