nearest-neighbor interactions much more complex. A detailed description of a GDCM model derivation for L12-structures is presented below. The formalism and theoretical background necessary for the derivation of the model has been taken from the previous model for B2-compounds.[79,80]

The crystal lattice of a fully ordered stoichiometric compound A3B with the cubic L12-structure can be divided into two sublattices: the A atoms occupy the face-centered positions constituting the a-sublattice, whereas the B atoms occupy the corner positions of the cube constituting the b-sublattice. In order to accommodate all possible combina- tions of point defects, both anti-structure atoms as well as vacancies are allowed on the two sublattices.

The total number of lattice sites in an intermetallic compound A3B with the ordered L12-structure is 2N, the numbers of A atoms, B atoms, and vacancies are NA, NB, and NV, respectively:

\begin{equation}
2N=N^{\alpha}+N^{\beta}=N_{A}+N_{B}+N_{V}
\end{equation}

where Na and Nb designate the total numbers of a- and b-sites with: Na ¼ ð3=2ÞN ; Nb ¼ ð1=2ÞN.

Consequently:

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{r}{N^{\alpha}=N_{A}^{\alpha}+N_{B}^{\alpha}+N_{V}^{\alpha}}\\ {N^{\beta}=N_{A}^{\beta}+N_{B}^{\beta}+N_{V}^{\beta}}\end{array}
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
(15)
\end{token}

where the superscript deﬁnes the sublattice and the subscript the corresponding species occupying it (with V ¼ vacancy). As energy parameters the pair-wise interaction energies between atoms and/or vacancies are taken into account which are regarded to be constant and independent of the occupancy of any other nearest-neighbor positions, and to be independent of the atom’s position in the crystal lattice. The total conﬁgurational energy E is the sum of pair-wise interaction energies between all atoms and vacancies:

E ¼ nAA"AA þ nBB"BB þ nVV"VV þ nAB"AB þ nAV"AV

þ nBV"BV

\begin{token}
(14)
\end{token}

\begin{token}
(16)
\end{token}

where eij are the nearest-neighbor pair-wise interaction energies between the species i and j (i, j ¼ A, B, or V), and nij are the corresponding numbers of pairs. For L12-phases it can be expressed as:

\begin{equation}
E=12\sum_{i}\left(n_{i A}E_{i A}+n_{i B}E_{i B}+n_{i V}E_{i V}\right)
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
(16a)
\end{token}

Here EiA, EiB, and EiV are the conﬁgurational energies contributed by the corresponding non-overlapping clusters (as deﬁned in ref.[104]) with an A atom, B atom, or vacancy in the central position.

Taking into account the possible distributions of A atoms, B atoms, and vacancies on the 12 a-sites belonging to one individual cluster, there are 312 ¼ 531 441 different defect containing cluster types, which contain an A atom, a B atom, or a vacancy in the central b-sublattice position. The number of clusters of type i with given numbers of A atoms (ai), B atoms (bi), and vacancies (ci) on the six a-sublattice sites is ni ¼ niA þ niB þ niV, and niA, niB, and niV are the corresponding fractions with either an A atom, a B atom, or a vacancy occupying the central b-site. A mean-ﬁeld approximation is introduced by assuming that the state of thermodynamic equilibrium is characterized by a set of equilibrium numbers of the different cluster types which are distributed over the N/12 lattice positions.

Each arrangement of the atoms on the lattice sites, corresponding to a given set of numbers of the different cluster types, is associated with the same total conﬁgurational energy E, the same total number of A atoms NA, and the same total number of B atoms NB.

Considering the expressions for the grand partition function (Eq. 3) and for the corresponding grand potential V (Eqs. 4–5), it can be assumed that the grand potential V depends on

