\begin{title}
8. Conclusions
\end{title}

The modeling and prediction of the behavior of inter- metallic compounds under various technological states could be of great practical importance. The outstanding properties of these compounds are closely related to the types of point defects present in the crystal lattice, mainly anti-structure defects or vacancies. Both thermal (intrinsic) defects and constitutional defects are responsible for the occurrence of non-stoichiometry. The impact of point defects bases on their critical presence in many metallurgical and solid-state phenomena. In this review, we tried to demonstrate the applicability of a quantitative structure–activity/property relationship method to modeling in materials science. To

describe properly the ordering process in an intermetallic system one has to consider the simultaneous occurrence of all possible sorts of point defects and their combinations in each of the sublattices. The statistical-thermodynamic formalism applied here is the grand canonical ensemble, which is especially adapted to the treatment of equilibria in open systems.

Statistical-thermodynamic modeling was used in combina- tion with experimental activity data to obtain information on type and concentrations of the point defects and degree of order. These modeling approaches permitted to identify data which are compatible with each other among different contradictory literature information (e.g., Ni3Al), and, for the ﬁrst time, to roughly estimate the defect formation energies for the intermetallic compounds Ni3Ga, Pt3In, and Pt3Ga. The approaches are in general suitable to estimate a degree of ordering, i.e., SRO or LRO, critical or transition temperature of order–disorder transformations, and distribution of point defects in the structure as it has been done for several compounds with B2-, and L12-structure.

\begin{title}
8.1. Addendum
\end{title}

The development of appropriate mathematical models of the phenomena of interest, the study of the analytical or numerical solutions of these models, and the investigation of their properties are at the foundation of research in modern science and a potential contribution to its philosophy. This applies particularly well, as was shown in this study, in materials science, for modeling defect structures in inter- metallic compounds.

Due to the good agreement between the experimental results and the theoretical predictions, the approaches presented in this review can in principle be recommended for an extension to model the structure and function of other more complex systems. These may also help address a challenge of rapid and accurate optimization in advanced solution of many fundamental problems, which are not able to be solved experimentally.

However, there is still much to do for improvement, optimization, and searching of new and more universal solutions.

