\begin{title}
Comparison of Interfacial Reactions Between AlSi7Mg and Alumina Filter After Casting and Spark Plasma Sintering**
\end{title}

By Anton Salomon,* Tilo Zienert, Claudia Voigt, Eva J€ackel, Olga Fabrichnaya, David Rafaja and Christos G. Aneziris

So-called “small impingement tests” were performed with alumina ceramic foam ﬁlters and melt of the aluminum alloy AlSi7Mg (A356). A second set of samples was produced using the “spark plasma sintering” (SPS). After solidiﬁcation, the resulting interface formation between metal melt and ceramic was compared to evaluate differences between the impingement test that is very close to industrial metal melt ﬁltration conditions and the powder metallurgical SPS process without convection of the liquid metal. The melt generation and interface formation during SPS was characterized by high heating rates of up to 1500 K min 1, holding times of 1 min up to 60 min and fast cooling. Using energy and wavelength dispersive X-ray spectroscopy, indications for spinel (MgAl2O4) were found at the original interface between AlSi7Mg melt and the alumina ﬁlter. Thermodynamic calculations were conducted and spinel formation was conﬁrmed. It was demonstrated that SPS could serve as a fast and reliable tool to facilitate the reactions that occur at metal melt/ceramic interfaces during ﬁltration.

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

The aim of the presented work was to investigate possible reactions of the aluminum alloy melt AlSi7Mg with alumina. The formation of reaction products at the metal/ceramic interface might contribute to the intended ﬁltration efﬁciency. Reaction between Mg-rich alloys and Al2O3 resulting in MgO formation is expected from thermodynamics and was observed experimentally at 600 °C.[1] According to thermody- namic consideration, further formation of spinel MgAl2O4 is possible. However, the concentration of Mg in AlSi7Mg alloys is low (0.3–0.6 mass%) and kinetics could be too slow for

[*] A. Salomon, T. Zienert, Dr. O. Fabrichnaya, Prof. D. Rafaja Institute of Materials Science, TU Bergakademie Freiberg, Gustav-Zeuner-Straße 5, 09599 Freiberg, Germany E-mail: anton.salomon@ww.tu-freiberg.de C. Voigt, Prof. C. G. Aneziris Institute of Ceramic, Glass- and Construction Materials, TU Bergakademie Freiberg, Agricolastraße 17, 09599 Freiberg, Germany E. J€ackel Foundry Institute, TU Bergakademie Freiberg, Bernhard-von- Cotta-Straße 4, 09599 Freiberg, Germany

[**] The authors would like to thank the German Research Foundation (DFG) for supporting the investigations in the projects A02, A03, A06, and S03 of the Collaborative Research Centre SFB 920.

above-mentioned reactions to occur at a casting temperature of 750 °C. Two different methods for the generation of liquid metal/ceramic interfaces were applied:

1. The so-called “small impingement test” was performed.[2] 2. The “spark plasma sintering” (SPS) was used. The SPS method (also known as ﬁeld assisted sintering technique (FAST) or pulsed electric current sintering (PECS)) is a powder metallurgical densiﬁcation/sintering method used for almost all kinds of materials. It is characterized by the simultaneous application of mechanical pressure along with a pulsed electric direct current that results in Joule heating of the sample (in the case of an electrical conducting powder) and of the tools (most often made from graphite). A current strength of several kA but comparatively low voltage of only few V is applied. SPS offers the possibility to reduce not only the processing time but in some cases even allows lowering the temperatures needed for the compaction of metals, ceramics, and composites. High heating and cooling rates (several hundred Kelvin per minute) and lower dwell times of only few minutes can be achieved. The resulting shorter processing time leads to a less pronounced grain growth and this in turn can improve mechanical, physical, or optical properties. The cleaning of powder particle surfaces is another beneﬁt that is ascribed to the applied electric current.[3–5]

The application of SPS for the generation of metal melts is new and the only process so far that made use of liquid metal

during SPS is the liquid inﬁltration for the production of carbon/carbon–silicon carbides.[6,7]

Various authors proved that liquid aluminum poorly wets alumina at the impingement testing temperature of 750 °C. A proof was the observed contact angle over 90°.[8–10] Bao et al.[8,9] reviewed literature concerning aluminum on alumina wetting and formulated an approach for the calculation of wetting angles at low temperatures. In these articles, it was also stated that the atmosphere plays a crucial role in the wetting behavior. The oxide skin on the aluminum melt leads to “anomalously high” contact angles and causes poor wetting.[8] This should also be the case during the impinge- ment testing and might have contributed to the poor wetting since these tests were performed under air and normal pressure.[8–10]

The wetting is also strongly inﬂuenced by the alloying elements. It was shown that Mg promotes the wetting and lowers the wetting temperature.[11,12] Additionally, the pres- ence of Mg in the alloy can lead to the formation of the spinel phase MgAl2O4 and this might promote the wetting even more.[11] Silicon does not have any inﬂuence on the wetting of aluminum alloys on alumina but it “increases the interfacial bond strength” and diffuses into alumina.[13]

In the ﬁrst part of this article, the experimental conditions and the results of the sample investigation will be presented. In a subsequent part, the results (especially of long-time SPS runs) will be validated using thermodynamic calculations.

\begin{title}
2. Experimental
\end{title}

\begin{title}
2.1. Materials and Experimental Preparation
\end{title}

The reticulated alumina ﬁlters used for the small impinge- ment test were produced according to ref.[2] The 20 ppi ﬁlters were composed of pure alumina after the sintering at 1600 °C.[2]

Bars of an aluminum alloy with Si and Mg as the main alloying elements referring to the European Standard EN AC – AlSi7Mg0.3 (A356) were melted and used for the impingement testing of these ﬁlters. Due to the high ductility of the aluminum alloy, it was not possible to obtain the alloy powder needed for SPS from the impingement test bars. For this reason, the composition differs slightly from the externally bought AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy powder used for SPS (see Table 1 for the composition of both powders).

For SPS, a special sample geometry was needed (see Section 2.3). Hollow cylinders with an outer diameter of

