20 mm, an inner diameter of approximately 15 mm, and length of 100 mm were prepared by slip casting. The alumina powder (Martoxid MR70, Albemarle Corporation, USA) was mixed with deionized water (40 mass%), a temporary binder (1 mass%, Optapix AC 170, Zschimmer & Schwarz, Germany), a liqueﬁer (0.6 mass%, Dolapix CE 64, Zschimmer & Schwarz) and a defoamer (0.15 mass%, Contraspum, Zschimmer & Schwarz). This mixture was homogenized in a ball mill for 3 h and poured into a plaster mould. As soon as the wanted wall thickness was reached (about 3 mm after approximately 20 min), the excess ceramic slurry was poured off. After drying on air and removal of the plaster mould, the cylinders were sintered (suspensory) in a three-step process: (i) heating up to 600 °C with 55 K h 1, 30 min dwell time; (ii) heating from 600 to 1600 °C with 140 K h 1, 60 min dwell time; (iii) cooling from 1600 to 800 °C with 140 K h 1, followed by free cooling.

In the same way, massive cylinders without inner cavities were produced. The hollow cylinders were cut diagonally into pieces with a height of approximately 20 mm. Platelets with a height of approximately 5 mm were cut from the massive cylinders with a diamond-tipped saw blade.

The aluminum alloy powder used for SPS was commercial- ly available alloy AlSi7Mg0.6 (TLS Technik, Germany) with a slightly higher amount of Mg in comparison to the alloy used for impingement testing. Table 1 summarizes the chemical composition and particle sizes of the two aluminum alloys.

Table 2 shows the composition and impurities of the powders used for the production of the two different shapes of alumina, namely the CFF and the hollow cylinders/plates.

\begin{title}
2.2. Small Impingement Tests
\end{title}

The “small impingement test” is a modiﬁed version of the impingement test that is in step with actual ﬁltration practice.[14] In contrast, the term “small impingement” refers to a smaller geometry of the ﬁlter (50 50 22 mm3) and to a lower amount of metal melt (3 kg of aluminum alloy were used). During this test, the 20 ppi ﬁlter is mechanically and thermally stressed. The aluminum melt is poured from a drop height of 300 mm over the CFF that is ﬁxed at the bottom of pouring basin. In this way, the thermal shock resistance of the ﬁlters is tested. This test was performed under normal pressure and air without feeder. The ceramic ﬁlter was not preheated. The test was passed when the ﬁlter did not break during casting. Afterwards, the metal melt was allowed to cool down and solidify. A detailed description of the small impingement test procedure is given in ref.[2]

\begin{caption}
Table 2. Composition of the alumina powders for the production of alumina CFF for impingement testing and cylinders/plates for SPS.
\end{caption}

\begin{table}
| ('', 'Shape of alumina', '')   | ('', 'Powder', '')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'Al2O3')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'Na2O')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'Fe2O3')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'SiO2')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'MgO')   | ('Composition of the powder [mass%]', '', 'CaO')   | ('', '', 'Experiment')   | ('', 'Particle', 'size')   | ('', '', 'Source')   |
