After impingement testing and solidiﬁcation of the metal, the inﬁltrated alumina ﬁlters were cut with a diamond-tipped saw blade, cold mounted in epoxy resin and ground and polished. The last step of the metallographic/ceramographic preparation consisted of vibration polishing of the samples for 24 h.

\begin{title}
2.3. Spark Plasma Sintering
\end{title}

Spark plasma sintering was performed in a SPS/FAST unit HPD 25 manufactured by FCT Systeme GmbH (Germany).

In another article that is featured in this special issue of Advanced Engineering Materials,[15] the SPS setup used also for this study was described in detail. A hollow cylinder made of the alumina ceramic was ﬁlled with the powder of the aluminum alloy. The bottom side of the cylinder was closed with a cylindrical, ﬂat plate made of alumina. This build-up was placed in the high-strength graphite tools of the SPS lined with graphite foils and the ensemble was thermally insulated using graphite felt. The geometries and materials of the whole conﬁguration are given in Table 3 (the graphite tooling parts were delivered by SGL Carbon, Germany).

Alumina is electrically isolating (speciﬁc electric resistance ranges from 1014 V cm at 225 °C to 108 V cm at 1000 °C[16]) and exhibits a low thermal conductivity (25 W m 1 K 1 at 100 °C to 7 W m 1 K 1 at 1000 °C[16]). Additionally, aluminum powders are hard to melt because of the Al2O3 passivation layer present in the rim of every powder particle. In turn, the current ﬂow through the entire sample itself during SPS is prevented, which would result in an increased current density in the surrounding

