graphite die and a heating of the whole sample geometry by the Joule heat created in the graphite.[3–5]

It is known from literature that the SPS process is capable of sintering aluminum powders by breaking the oxide layer leading to the sintering and compaction of pure aluminum. The main inﬂuential parameters for this compaction process are pressure, sintering temperature, and the composition of the aluminum alloy.[17–20]

Using the setup introduced in ref.,[15] the alloy was molten without the application of an external load. The sample setup allowed for the upper graphite piston to stay in contact with the powder. This was necessary to achieve the melting of the passivated aluminum powder particles by the electric current that (partly) entered through the piston and the graphite foil.

For the melting process itself, an electric pulse pattern of the direct electric current pulses was used. It consisted of pulses with a duration of 10 ms and a pause duration of 5 ms. The number of pulse patterns was chosen to be 1 and no additional pause was applied. More details about the current pulsing in the particular SPS/FAST unit HPD 25 are given in ref.[21] High heating rates of 1000–1500 K min 1 were used to reach the desired melt temperature of 750 °C (as it was used for impingement testing). The temperature was measured with an optical pyrometer focussed on the inside of the drill-hole in the upper graphite piston only few millimeters above the sample. The pyrometer-controlled sintering process starts at 450 °C (just above the beginning of the pyrometer detection range) which is the reason why the deﬁned heating rates could only be

applied from there on. The initial heating up to 450 °C is current controlled with 45% of the maximum electrical power within 1 min. The dwell time at 750 °C lasted between 1 min (corresponding to the short-time impingement) and 60 min whereas the longest dwell times were chosen as reference values and were not based on a technical application. Afterwards, the power was shut off and rapid cooling by the water cooled upper and lower piston was started until room temperature was reached. The whole process ran under vacuum (absolute pressure of about 5 10 2–5 10 3 mbar).

The samples were sand blasted, cut with a diamond-tipped saw blade and cold mounted in embedding medium. Subsequently, the samples were ground and polished accord- ing to the established procedures (metallography of the Institute of Materials Science) for aluminum alloys. Every sample was additionally treated using vibration polishing for 24 h.

\begin{title}
2.4. Analytical Methods
\end{title}

To get a ﬁrst impression of the sintering results, optical microscopy (Neophot 30, Carl Zeiss AG, Germany) was used.

A high-resolution scanning electron microscope (SEM) LEO-1530 (Carl Zeiss AG) equipped with ﬁeld emission cathode and an energy dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDX) was used for investigating the interfaces in detail. The measurements were carried out at an acceleration voltage of 18 kV.

Electron probe microanalysis (EPMA) line scans were performed with a JXA 8900 RL equipped with ﬁve crystal spectrometers and additional EDX. The step size for this analysis was chosen to be 0.5 mm.

\begin{title}
3. Thermodynamic Modeling
\end{title}

\begin{title}
3.1. Thermodynamic Models
\end{title}

The calculations of thermodynamic equilibrium are based on the Gibbs energy minimization of the multiphase multicomponent system

\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{m i n}\left(G\right)=\operatorname*{m i n}\left(\sum_{\alpha}m^{\alpha}G_{\mathrm{m}}^{\alpha}\left(T,p,x_{i}^{\alpha}\right)\right)
\end{equation}

where ma is the amount of phase a, Ga m is the molar Gibbs energy depending on pressure p, temperature T, and mole fraction xa i of the constituent i in phase a. The minimization of the Gibbs energy enables one to determine which phases are stable at given p, T, and bulk composition of the system as well as phase compositions. The calculations are made using the Thermo-Calc program set.[22] The same program set is used to construct equilibrium phase diagrams and to make Scheil simulation.

The Gibbs energy of stoichiometric phases is described by

\begin{equation}
G=\sum_{i}x_{i}G_{i}+a+b\cdot T
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
