\end{token}

or if cP data is available as

\begin{equation}
G=a+b\cdot T+c\cdot T\ln T+d\cdot T^{2}+e\cdot T^{-1}
\end{equation}

\begin{title}
where xi is the mole fraction of element i.
\end{title}

Solid solution phases are described with the compound energy formalism, where the atoms can occupy different sublattices related to the crystal structure.[23] In case of a two sublattice model ði1; i2; i3; . . .ÞI nðj1; j2; j3; . . .ÞII m with the stoichio- metric numbers of each sublattice n and m the Gibbs energy is given as

\begin{equation}
G=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}y_{i}^{\mathrm{I}}y_{j}^{\mathrm{II}}G_{i:j}+RT\left[n\sum_{i}y_{i}^{\mathrm{I}}\ln y_{i}^{\mathrm{I}}+m\sum_{j}y_{j}^{\mathrm{II}}\ln y_{j}^{\mathrm{II}}\right]
\end{equation}

where i and j are the atoms or ions, y is the site fractions of the atoms in the sublattice, Gi:j is the Gibbs energy of the end member, GE is the excess Gibbs energy and Tis the temperature in K.

Metallic liquid phase is described by substitutional model with the Gibbs energy expressed as

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}G=&\sum_{i}x_{i}G_{i}+RT\sum_{i}x_{i}\ln x_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n}x_{i}x_{j}L_{ij}\\&+\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1}\sum_{k=j+1}^{n}x_{i}x_{j}x_{k}L_{ijk}\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
ð5Þ
\end{token}

where xi and xj are mole fractions of components i and j, respectively, while Lij and Lijk are binary and ternary mixing parameters, respectively.

\begin{title}
3.2. Thermodynamic Description
\end{title}

The thermodynamic calculations are performed using a thermodynamic database, which was combined from several works. Unary data are taken from Dinsdale.[24] The metallic alloy is modeled by the Al–Mg–Si–Fe system. The thermo- dynamic parameters of this system were optimized using CALPHAD approach and the phase modeling is described in details elsewhere.[25,26] The ceramic material was modeled by the Al2O3–MgO–SiO2 system. The binary system Fe–O was not included, because the iron oxide phases (wustite, magnetite, and hematite) are not stable at the substantially low oxygen partial pressure used to simulate experimental conditions. The binary description of the Si–O system including three polymorphic modiﬁcations of SiO2 (quartz, tridymite, and cristobalite) is taken from Taylor and Dinsdale[27] and Hallstedt.[28,29] The Mg–O description with stoichiometric MgO compound is from Hallstedt.[30] The Al–O description including stoichiometric phase Al2O3, corundum, is accepted from Taylor et al.[31] and Hallstedt.[29] The thermodynamic description of the quasi-binary system of MgO–SiO2 is from

