\begin{title}
4. Experimental Results and Discussion
\end{title}

\begin{title}
4.1. Impingement Testing
\end{title}

In correspondence with ref.,[2] all of the tested ﬁlters passed the “small impingement test” without breaking. The ability to withstand the thermal shock needed for being used in aluminum melt ﬁltration was demonstrated.

After solidiﬁcation and metallographic/ceramographic sample preparation, optical microscopy was used to check the wetting of the aluminum alloy AlSi7Mg0.3 in contact with the ceramic. In general, the wetting of the aluminum alloy on the alumina ﬁlter was found to be poor. As it can be seen in Figure 1, an interlayer of epoxy resin was found to exist after the cold mounting of the samples. The resin had ﬂown in the existing gap between the ceramic and the metal that had developed due to the poor wetting. Additionally, the enormous differences in the thermal expansions have to be mentioned (ath, 20–1000°C (Al2O3) ¼ 8.3 10 6 K 1 [16] in contrast to ath, 0 °C (pure aluminum) ¼ 24 10 6 K 1 [36]) which contribute to the gap size.

There were only few regions of the ﬁlter, where the solidiﬁed aluminum alloy was in direct contact with the ceramic ﬁlter struts. One of these regions (Figure 2a) was selected for the analysis of the local chemical composition. Despite the presence of Mg and Si in the alloy, the SEM pictures (see Figure 2a) revealed that there were no detectable interfacial reactions or intermediate phases between AlSi7Mg0.3 alloy and the alumina ceramic after the solidiﬁcation and short-time contact of the metal melt with the ceramic ﬁlter. As anticipated due to the absence of open porosity, no penetration of the metal

\begin{legend}
b
\end{legend}

melt into the ceramic was found. This is consistent with the results of other researchers that investigated the interfacial reactions of pure aluminum with alumina and did not ﬁnd a reaction layer or penetration of the melt as well.[10,11,37–39]

EDX mapping at the interfacial region showed the presence of silicon needles in the vicinity of the interface (Figure 2b). The formation of these Si needles is attributed to the cooling conditions and the poor thermal conductivity of alumina. The phase relations in the alloy AlSi7Mg0.3 were thoroughly investigated by Zienert and Fabrichnaya.[26] Combining experimental and thermodynamic considerations, the authors showed the existence of Mg2Si, t6-AlFeSi, and p-phase (Al8Si6Mg3Fe) besides fcc-Al and fcc-Si after solidiﬁcation. The existence of Fe-containing t6- and p-phase was not conﬁrmed by EDX in the present work. However, precip- itations containing both Mg and Si were detected (not shown in Figure 2). Thus, the presence of Mg2Si was assumed besides pure silicon and aluminum.

\begin{title}
4.2. SPS Treatment of the Samples
\end{title}

Since the pyrometer detection range starts at 400 °C, it takes only 12–18 s to heat the sample to the desired temperature of 750 °C, exceeding the melting temperature of the aluminum alloy. Especially in this temperature range, the response of the pyrometer-controlled heating is slow, which causes large overheating of up to 60 K at the heating rate of 1500 K min 1. This overheating was signiﬁcantly reduced, when the heating rate was decreased to 1000 K min 1. Therefore, the reduced heating rate was applied for all experiments performed in this work.

The successful melting of the aluminum alloy powder could immediately be seen on the cross-section of the sample (Figure 3). In most of the cases, the aluminum melt adhered to the ceramic body and a direct contact could be achieved. Figure 3 shows a photograph of one of these samples after cold mounting and surface polishing (side view of the cut sample).

\begin{caption}
Fig. 3. Sample after cutting, cold mounting in epoxy resin and metallographic preparation.
\end{caption}

Because of the melting of the powder particles and the shrinkage during cooling and solidiﬁcation, the cavity in the ceramic is not entirely ﬁlled, but the metal covers, at least partly, the ceramic. In this particular case, a small piece of the ceramic under study had been placed inside the aluminum to force the interface formation in a sort of a press ﬁt.

The melting of the alloy seems to start in the powder particles that are initially in contact with the graphite foil and piston, and then to propagate until the whole powder is molten. This can be attributed to the electric current that enters the initially present aluminum powder supporting the melting of the powder particles by the internal Joule heating and destruction of the surface oxides (as described in refs.[17–20]) in addition to the heat acting from the outside of the sample due to the current passing through the graphite die and the Joule heating created there.

The wetting of the alumina ﬁlter by the aluminum alloy AlSi7Mg0.6 was checked with optical microscopy. Figure 4 shows a typical micrograph of the region near the interface between the alumina and the alloy. In combination with SEM micrographs (see Figure 5a), it was shown that secondary phases gather at the interface. The regions with a direct contact were mostly located in the upper half of the samples, which is a result of the melting starting in this part of the specimen. In ref.,[11] the authors found that the wetting angle at 800 °C is reduced by <5° when increasing the Mg content from 0 to 1 mass% in the system Al–Mg/Al2O3. Thus, the increase of Mg content from 0.3 mass% in AlSiMg0.3 used for impingement to 0.6 mass% in AlSi7Mg0.6 used for SPS is too low to have a signiﬁcant inﬂuence on the wetting (preliminary SPS experi- ments with swarf of AlSiMg0.3 alloy had supported these results). At the joint interfaces between aluminum melt and alumina, no gaps or cavities were found. Based on their experimental results, Shen et al.[10] concluded that any disruption or reduction of the oxide ﬁlm covering the aluminum particles contributes to the improvement on the wettability between the aluminum alloys and the alumina. Thus, the removal of the passivation oxide layer on the

