\begin{caption}
Fig. 5. (a) SEM (BSE contrast) of the aluminum alloy/alumina interface, pores and one diamond (remnant from metallography) are visible as well as secondary phases; (b) EDX mapping of the interfacial region in (a), Si needles and ternary phases with Fe and Mg.
\end{caption}

aluminum powder during the SPS process causes lower wetting angels and better wettability. In addition, the formation of new surface oxides is hindered by the low oxygen partial pressure (vacuum atmosphere) and reducing conditions due to the presence of graphite tools.

The elimination of the thin alumina ﬁlm in the vicinity of the melting point of aluminum was explained by the production of a gaseous suboxide.[40] Based on their experimental results, Laurent et al.[40] concluded that liquid aluminum in the vicinity of the melting point reacts with the oxide layer and forms the gas Al2O. According to their ﬁndings, only a “disrupted,” thin ﬁlm with a thickness of a few monolayers is affected by this mechanism.[40] However, no gas formation was detected during the SPS treatment.

Figure 5a shows the interface between the aluminum alloy and the alumina with BSE (backscattered electron) contrast. Some pores are visible and a diamond particle (black) that got stuck in the highly ductile aluminum during grinding and polishing. No intermediate layer except for the accumulation of Si near the interface was found. An EDX mapping on the same interface is shown in Figure 5b. This analysis proved the absence of a reaction zone. However, silicon needles were found to gather at the alumina surface. This effect was already described in literature for a high Si containing alloy.[13] According to the results of Sulima and Mikulowski[13] for the alloy AlSi11, Si crystals were present at the alloy/alumina interface that did not affect the wettability but improved bond strength of the joint interface. This increase of shear strength was explained by the fact that Si “diffused through the interface into alumina substrate” and caused “structure changes.”[13] However, no such diffusion of Si was found for the short-time SPS treated samples in our study (see Figure 5b). Mg and Fe (as an impurity) were found to be present in some of the Si containing precipitations. According to the thermodynamic calculations (see below), the ternary t6-phase (AlFeSi) as well as Mg2Si should be formed.

The results described above are valid for the short dwell time (1 min at 750 °C) experiments that were aimed to be close to the conditions of impingement testing and actual ﬁltration practice. Long time SPS experiments with dwell times from 30 to 60 min at a temperature of 750 °C were conducted to evaluate possible interfacial reactions. The other sintering/ melting parameters remained unchanged (heating rate of 1000 K min 1, free cooling, vacuum).

An EDX map of a sharp edged alumina piece within the aluminum alloy is shown in Figure 6. The sample had been held at 750 °C for 30 min. In agreement with Figure 5b, the gathering of Si-rich needles at the interface was found again. Moreover, a local increase of the Mg concentration at the metal/ceramic interface was detected. Although no distinctive reaction layer was detected, the local enrichment of Mg concentration was a ﬁrst hint for a possible spinel (MgAl2O4) formation as described in ref.[11] The EDX results from Figure 6 were validated with WDX/EPMA line scans (wavelength dispersive X-ray spectroscopy).

Figure 7a shows a SEM micrograph (InLens/SE detector) of the metal/ceramic interface of sample that had been held at 750 °C for 60 min. The picture was taken after the EPMA analysis, thus the contamination zone is visible that marks the trace of the electron beam. The Si-rich needles are visible even in this topographic contrast. No observable reaction layer was found to exist.

The corresponding EPMA line scans measured at a step size of 0.5 mm are summarized in Figure 7b. Due to the size of the electron beam and the interaction volume (few mm3) the small step size was mainly employed to improve the statistics of the EPMA/WDX analysis. The measurement started in the bulk alumina, crossed the interface and ended in the aluminum alloy. As expected, a penetration of the melt into the dense ceramic could not be found. Again, Mg (pink line) was detected directly at the alumina/alloy interface only, which indicates a pre-stage or the beginning of the spinel formation.

