The Si-rich needles, observed in the SEM image (Figure 7a), were detected with WDX as well and are marked by the peaks in the Si curve (green line in Figure 7b). These local maxima of Si concentration coincide with local minima of the Al concentration (red curve). Adjacent to the transition region, which extends between approximately 20 and 60 mm in Figure 7a, a region of almost pure aluminum follows. This transition region can precede the formation of a reaction zone. Impurities like iron, etc. are not shown in Figure 7b.

These results of the EDX maps and the EPMA line scans were veriﬁed on several samples with dwell times of 30 and 60 min. In all samples under study, Mg was found on every alumina/aluminum alloy interface. According to ref.,[11] a pre- stage of the MgAl2O4 spinel formation is assumed to be responsible for the increase of Mg concentration at the Al2O3/AlSi7Mg interfaces.

\begin{title}
5. Thermodynamic Calculations
\end{title}

Thermodynamic calculations were done for the composi- tion of the AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy reduced to the Al–Fe–Mg–Si

forming from the starting materials. In addition, a phase fraction versus temperature diagram presented in Figure 10 was calculated between 20 and 1000 °C. It can be seen that the spinel phase was calculated to be stable over the whole temperature range. In contrast to the results calculated for the pure AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy no Mg2Si phase was solidiﬁed from the melt. This shows that all magnesium available in the alloy in solid and liquid state reacts with alumina to form spinel phase. The t6-AlFeSi phase was solidiﬁed from the last melt at 576.4 °C in very small amounts.

\begin{title}
6. Summary and Conclusions
\end{title}

So-called “small impingement tests” were performed with ceramic foam ﬁlters made from alumina and with molten aluminum alloy AlSi7Mg0.3. The wetting behavior and the resulting metal/ceramic interfaces were investigated. A second series of samples was produced using the SPS, for which a special setup was developed to melt aluminum alloy powder AlSi7Mg0.6. The wetting behavior was found to be different in the respective process.

system (in mass%: Al 92.228, Si 7.07, Mg 0.61, and Fe 0.092). In Figure 8, the calculated phase fraction versus temperature diagram is shown. This diagram shows the amount of phases formed in case of thermodynamic equilibrium conditions. The Mg2Si disappears at 557.2 °C on heating with the beginning of melting, while t6 and (Si) disappear at 571.2 and 572.2 °C, respectively. The amount of fcc, Al solid solution, decreases between 557.2 and 613.7 °C until the transformation to liquid phase is completed.

In Figure 9, the result of the Scheil simulation of reactions taking place in AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy during casting is presented. The Scheil simulation is based on the assump- tion of local equilibrium and that there is inﬁnitely fast diffusion in liquid phase and no diffusion in solid phases. The Scheil approach provides more realistic description of solidiﬁ- cation of the alloy during the SPS process than the equilibrium calculations. The comparison of the results of the Scheil-solidiﬁcation presented in Figure 9 with equilibrium calculations (Figure 8) indicates only small differences in temperature of the phase pre- cipitation for that alloy composition.

Calculation of the reactions occurring between the AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy and the alumina ﬁlter was done for the composition consisting of 0.5 mol of the alloy and 0.5 mol of Al2O3. Results of equilibrium calculations at 400 and 750 °C are shown in Table 4. The equilibrium calculation indicates stability of small amounts of spinel phase MgAl2O4

