The SPS treatment resulted in samples with a better wetting of the aluminum alloy on the alumina in comparison to the small impingement tests. However, for dwell times of 1min at 750°C, the impingement testing results were reproduced using SPS: the

\begin{caption}
Fig. 10. Calculated phase fraction versus temperature diagram between the AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy and alumina.
\end{caption}

liquid metal did not penetrate the ceramic surface and no intermediate layer or other reaction products were formed. However, an increase of the holding times to 30–60min led to the increase of Mg concentration at the interface between alumina and AlSi7Mg0.6 alloy as detected with EDX mapping and EPMA line scans. The formation of a reaction layer was still not found but a possible pre-stage of spinel (MgAl2O4) was assumed.

This hypothesis was supported by thermodynamic calcu- lations. According to the presented thermodynamic calcula- tions, all available magnesium in the alloy in solid and liquid state reacts with alumina to form the spinel phase MgAl2O4 that is thermodynamically stable in the temperature range between 20 and 1000 °C.

It can be assumed that the presence of the spinel phase at the ﬁlter surface alters the ﬁltration efﬁciency of the alumina ﬁlter. However, it could be shown that long dwell times of about 1 h and convection-less conditions would be necessary to force the formation of a continuous reaction layer. At the ﬁltering conditions used for the impingement testing, the spinel MgAl2O4 does not form.

