boundaries.[3–5] With the introduction of directionally solidi- ﬁed (DS) and single-crystal (SX) superalloys, the allowable turbine rotor inlet temperature increased to 1427 °C (2600 F) over the last 30 years. However, precision investment casting methods for producing hot-section DS and SX components are expensive and thus EQ Ren e 80 still remains a suitable candidate for various hot-section components in aircraft and industrial gas turbines.

For EQ alloys, optimization of the microstructure involves optimizing the grain size, and increasing resistance to creep and fatigue loading by optimizing the heat treatment steps. The grain size is a very important parameter that governs the mechanical properties. According to the Hall–Petch relation- ship,[6] reﬁnement of the grain size will result in a higher yield strength and toughness for the alloy. The additional advan- tage of utilizing secondary phase precipitates derives from the achievement of very high yield strengths due to dislocation retardation up to high homologous temperatures in precipi- tation-hardened nickel-based superalloys. However, EQ alloys are also more prone to grain boundary sliding and creep void formations since the grain boundaries are oriented in random directions and more grain boundary area is susceptible to transverse loading than in DS or SX alloys. Larger grain sizes in EQ alloys are beneﬁcial from a creep strength perspective due to the reduction of overall grain boundary area. Under rapid heating, the grain boundary phases are unable to dissolve fully into the surrounding matrix and their partial dissolution leads to the formation of a low melting point eutectic and melting of the grain boundary region.[7] Rush et al.[8] showed that an increase in the grain size lowers the ductility and causes more segregation of melting point depressing elements at the grain boundary, resulting in an increase in average crack length in the cold metal transfer process, where material was welded in the solutionized state. This was attributed to laser power since the application of higher laser power results in more area being liquated, thus the temperature gradient decreases and larger grains start forming. However, this might also result in re- solidiﬁcation at lower temperatures and a change in the nature of the stress from tensile to compressive. Hence, constitutional liquation, that is, grain boundary g0 dissolution and formation of g/g0 eutectic is more detrimental and results in more liquation cracking.[5] It has been found that high Ti and Al concentrations in superalloys lead to signiﬁcant amounts of strain–age cracking and hence, many of these alloys are labeled “non-weldable.” Ren e 80 is one such superalloy.[9]

Vickers microhardness values around 450 were reported for Ren e 80 away from the bonding zone in transient liquid phase diffusion bonding.[10] Osoba et al. reported microhard- ness values at different heat treatment conditions and correlated them with microﬁssuring tendency. The highest value obtained was near 420 HV.[7] DS Ren e 80 has a higher concentration of Al þ Ti, the major elements of the hardening phase, which results in its higher hardness (440 5 HV). However, differences in the characteristics of the base metal-

grain boundary interaction in the heat-affected zone (HAZ) also resulted in lower numbers of cracks for DS Ren e 80 compared to EQ Ren e 80.[11]

Attempts to repair Ren e 80 components started in the late sixties. Different welding techniques such as activated diffusion bonding involving vacuum furnace brazing, and diffusion and aging heat treatments were attempted.[12] Since the braze alloys used in furnace brazing are of a different composition than the base metal, epitaxy cannot be main- tained and the joint strength is poor. The application of fusion welding processes to precipitation hardened nickel base superalloys containing higher volume percentage of g0 (30– 50%) like Ren e 80 has been severely limited because of their high susceptibility to HAZ cracking during welding and post- weld treatment.[13] With the advent of laser cladding processes, repair of components made of the “non-weldable” category of superalloys has been attempted.[14] Recently, electron beam melting was shown to be capable of depositing monolithic structures of Ren e 142 superalloy.[15] Micro scale processes with low laser heat input were shown to be successful for IN 100 superalloy through a rigorous design of experiments-based study.[16] Although these methods have seen some success, this method of repair has typically been plagued by crack formation as well as grain multiplication that occurs when the dendrite arms are separated from their primary dendrites due to remelting. The powder blowing mechanism used in cladding processes leads to two signiﬁ- cant problems. The ﬁrst problem, associated with stray grain formation, is due to the disturbance of convective currents in the melt pool caused by the high velocity powder particles entering the melt, adversely impacting DS and SX micro- structure formation. The second problem, associated with crack formation, is the limitation that a mechanical powder feed assembly imposes on the speed at which the energy source can scan across the substrate. It has typically been found that higher scan speeds lead to reduced crack and stray grain formation but the speed at which the energy source moves relative to the substrate in typical laser cladding or directed energy deposition processes is signiﬁcantly limited by the X–Y stage on which the substrate or the energy source is mounted. To date, very few methods have claimed success for non-weldable superalloys such as Ren e 80[17] and the authors did not ﬁnd any signiﬁcant contributions demonstrating crack-free deposits of Ren e 80.

The work on scanning laser epitaxy (SLE) in the present investigation is a direct descendant of a metal powder bed- based processes referred to as direct selective laser sintering (SLS) developed in the late 1990s.[18] SLE extends the work on direct SLS by allowing for the direct layer-by-layer construc- tion of net-shape engine-ready components that have speciﬁed EQ, DS, or SX microstructure, which may not require hot isostatic pressing for further densiﬁcation. In SLE, a tightly focused laser beam is guided by high-speed galvanometer scanners enabling tight control over the amount of energy being applied to the top of the pre-placed powder bed, as well as the speed at which the melt pool moves across

the substrate. Under the proper operating conditions and with sufﬁcient substrate meltback, the melted powder undergoes epitaxial solidiﬁcation.[18] The solidiﬁcation microstructure in the new deposit thus follows the microstructural morphology of the underlying substrate, allowing for EQ, DS, and even SX growth.[19]

The work presented here describes SLE as a repair technology applied to high g0 containing EQ Ren e 80 superalloy and demonstrates the capability of SLE to produce dense, crack-free deposits. A custom code based on active contouring has been developed to perform quantitative metallography and process parameter optimization based on microstructural information such as voids, deposit uniformity, and melt depth. The composition and structure of the deposits were investigated using SEM and TEM to understand the microstructure processing–property relation- ships. Microhardness indentation was carried out in the substrate, weld-interface, and the deposit regions.

\begin{title}
2. Experimental Section
\end{title}

The key pieces of the experimental set-up involved in the SLE process are a single mode, 1 kW Ytterbium ﬁber laser (IPG Photonics, model:YLS-1000), a 3-axis galvanometer scanning system (Cambridge Technology), a custom-built controlled atmosphere processing chamber, a thermal imaging camera (Mikron MCS 640), and a custom-built melt pool video microscope (Control Vision, Inc.). The ﬁber laser is focused through the galvanometer scanner to a spot size of 40 mm Gaussian beam diameter, enabling tight control of the temperature conditions surrounding the melt pool. All samples were run with a ﬁxed scan spacing of 12.7 mm. The scanner has the capability of moving the laser beam at speeds of up to several m s 1, though typical scan speeds used in SLE are on the order of hundreds of mm s 1. The SLE process takes place inside a controlled-atmosphere process chamber purged to less than 10 parts-per-million (PPM) oxygen at atmospheric pressure. Both the powder and the substrate, described below, are initially at ambient tempera- ture and no external heating is used to bias the powder or substrate temperature during processing.

