\begin{title}
2.2. Methods
\end{title}

\begin{title}
2.2.1. Design-of-Experiments (DoE)-Based Study
\end{title}

A raster scan pattern across the width of the powder atop each coupon was used to propagate a linear melt pool along the coupon length. Three variables were altered between each experimental run: laser power, scan speed, and the number of initial repeated scans at the start of the raster pattern. The laser power and scan speed of the scan were both used to control the amount of energy being applied to the sample during the raster scan pattern. The number of initial repeated scans affects the formation of a melt pool at the start of the substrate as well as the preheating of the substrate. Sufﬁcient repeat scanning is required in order to pre-heat the underlying substrate and form a proper fusion bond across the whole substrate. Without pre-heat the melt pool does not wet the surface of the underlying substrate and voids are formed along the weld-interface. The zigzag raster scan pattern with alternate scan vector is illustrated in Figure 1(b) for coupon placed in position 2. Here, after each pass the laser moves over by the raster scan step distance and moves in the opposite direction. The DoEs conducted were phase 1 ascent DoEs based on a fractional factorial design.[20]

\begin{title}
2.2.2. Optical Microscopy
\end{title}

