\begin{title}
2.2.3. Quantitative Image Analysis
\end{title}

Each sectioned and polished specimen was analyzed to determine the height of the deposited material, the uniformity of the deposit height, the amount of melt back along the substrate, and the locations of cracks or porosity. A customized image analysis code was written in MATLAB to extract all the metrics that are essential for the DoE-based process parameter optimization.[21] Defects found with high circularity values are labeled as pores and those found with low circularity values are labeled as cracks.

\begin{title}
2.2.4. Microindentation Hardness
\end{title}

Microindentation hardness test was carried out using a Buehler microhardness indenter with load ﬁxed at 500 gf. The hardness values are reported in Vicker’s microhardness scale (HV).

\begin{title}
2.2.5. SEM
\end{title}

To understand the microstructural composition and to characterize the newly formed Ren e 80 deposits, investigations were carried out using LEO-1550 and FEI XL-40 SEMs. Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) was carried out and is reported. For further investigation, g0 etchant (composition: 100mL H2O þ 100 mL HClþ 100 mL HNO3 þ 3 g MoO3) was utilized. This etchant preferentially attacks the g0 precipitate phase, leaving the residual gamma channels and former location of precipitates visible upon metallographic inspection.

\begin{title}
2.2.6. TEM
\end{title}

FIB was performed with an FEI Strata DB 235 dual beam FIB/SEM equipped with a liquid metallorganic ion source (LIMS) for protective Pt layer deposition. Bulk samples were extracted from the deposit and substrate regions of samples, and were sufﬁciently far from the deposit/substrate interface so as to not remove material from the heat-affected zone. The sample was thinned with the ion beam to a maximum of 100 nm thickness, and then removed from the SEM chamber

for TEM analysis. TEM analysis was performed using a JEOL 2010F high resolution TEM (HR-TEM). Bright ﬁeld TEM (BF-TEM) was performed for general microstructural charac- terization and secondary g0 precipitate size analysis.

\begin{title}
2.2.7. Post-Weld Heat Treatment Study
\end{title}

Selected Ren e 80 samples were cut in half lengthwise. One half was polished for metallographic analysis while the other half was subjected to thermal stress relief heat treatment at 1082 °C in an argon-purged tube furnace for approximately 2 h including heating and cooling time. The heat-treated sections were analyzed through metallography to conﬁrm whether or not strain–age cracking was present in post-weld heat-treated specimens.

