3. Results and Discussion
\end{title}

\begin{title}
3.1. Metallographic Examination
\end{title}

Figure 2 shows the transverse microstructure of an SLE-processed Ren e 80 sample. As can be seen from Figure 2 (a), an excellent metallurgical bond is formed between the substrate and the deposit region and the deposit is crack-free and dense. Although there was no indication of cracking in the deposited material, several of the Ren e 80 samples showed evidence of spherical porosity, near the top of the sample, as illustrated in Figure 2(a). The deposit region also shows a ﬁner microstructure compared to the substrate region as shown in Figure 2(b). The microstructural reﬁnement can be attributed to the localized heating and fast cooling due to laser heat source application. The microstructure of the deposited region consists of dendrite segregation patterns (Figure 2(c)). Due to the segregation of heavy elements such as W and Mo at the core of dendrite and enrichment of the Al and Ti elements in the inter-dendritic region, the etching made the dendrite region bright and the inter-dendritic region gray.[4]

