The set of metrics used to develop process maps for the Ren e 80 samples included the average deposit height, average melt back depth, and deposit uniformity. The deposit uniformity was calculated by taking the deposit height of the sample, developing a linear ﬁt for the height and then calculating the average R2 residual between the height and the linear ﬁt. The higher the R2 value, the more uniform the deposit. Figure 8(a) illustrates the calculation of the deposit height uniformity metric.

It was found that all samples exhibiting average melt back depth below 100 mm had a lack of fusion at a minimum of one

point along the sample length. For this reason, an average melt depth of 100 mm was chosen as the lower limit in the process maps developed. Figure 8(b) shows an overview of the effects of the processing parameters on each metric.

It can be seen from the proﬁles that laser power most signiﬁcantly impacts the deposit height and the melt depth and tends to signiﬁcantly increase each of these values as it increases. Scan speed most signiﬁcantly impacts the melt depth and causes it to decrease as the scan speed increases. The numbers of repeat scans has a signiﬁcant impact on the deposit uniformity and melt depth, decreasing both as the repeat scans increase. As can be seen, reducing the number of repeat scans and laser power leads to more uniform deposits with reasonable melt depth. Reducing either these variables below certain thresholds can result in signiﬁcant balling in the melted powder or lack of fusion between the deposit and the underlying coupons. For reference, the highest quality sample produced in these trials had a deposit uniformity R2 value of 0.65 and the lowest quality samples had uniformities on the order of 0.20. It was also found that very high scan speeds in conjunction with high laser power will lead to substrate warping due to thermal stresses in the substrate. These phenomena would likely set an upper bound on the process map.

\begin{title}
4. Conclusion
\end{title}

The SLE process was investigated for its use in the additive repair of Ni-superalloy turbine engine hot-section compo- nents. The process was found to be capable of producing high uniformity, low melt-back deposits of Ren e 80 material on like-chemistry substrates without any indications of cracking. The high g0 content of Ren e 80 and the presence of MC carbides are considered the main reasons for this alloy’s high crack-susceptibility. The lack of cracking seen in the SLE process is due to the high amount of pre-heat and post-heat applied to each portion of the sample due to beam overlapping during successive passes of the laser’s raster scan. This along with the controlled heat input and fast movement of the laser beam enabled successful fabrication of Ren e 80 deposits atop like-chemistry substrates without the need of ﬁller material having a signiﬁcantly altered composi- tion relative to the substrate. In SLE, a very ﬁnely focused Gaussian beam of diameter 40 microns performs raster scans across the powder, thus localizing the energy source. The use of a galvanometer scanner for rapidly directing the beam motion allows a range of scan speeds to be used. Together, these features enable wider range of processing conditions for different geometrical requirements. The melt pool width can also be monitored by the thermal imaging camera in real time and controlled within a range from the beam diameter to several hundreds of microns depending on the scan parameters used (laser power, scan speed, scan spacing). This imparts signiﬁcant capability to the SLE process for complex 3D geometry creation and is a focus of ongoing studies.

Microstructural investigations revealed the formation of g– g0 matrix with ﬁner microstructural features in the deposit region. SEM and TEM studies showed the presence of MC carbides, primary and secondary g0 particles and the segregated dendritic microstructure. The secondary g0 showed a bimodal distribution in the deposit region. The ﬁner microstructure in the deposit region resulted in a higher microhardness relative to the substrate. Post-weld heat treatment did not produce any cracks in the heat-treated samples. However, the effects of heat treatment on micro- structural reﬁnement, creep properties and g0 modiﬁcation are the subject of ongoing studies, and will be reported in future work.

