\begin{title}
Theoretical Combined Experimental Study of Unique He Behaviors in High-Entropy Alloys
\end{title}

Jutao Hu,§ Jianwei Zhang,§ Jinjing Zhang, Haiyan Xiao,* Lei Xie, Huahai Shen,* Pengcheng Li, Guangai Sun, and Xiaotao Zu

Cite This: Inorg. Chem. 2021, 60, 1388−1397

\begin{title}
ACCESS
\end{title}

ABSTRACT: Exploring new structural materials with strong He damage tolerance is one of the key tasks for the development of nuclear reactors. Helium (He), one of the most common elements in the nuclear environment, often forms undesired bubbles in metallic materials and may result in void swelling as well as high-temperature intergranular embrittlement. In this study, the behaviors of He in high- entropy alloy (HEA) TiZrHfMoNb and its constituents are systemati- cally investigated both theoretically and experimentally. Density functional theory calculations show that the He atom prefers to occupy tetrahedral and octahedral interstitial sites in a HEA. The migration pathway for He in TiZrHfMoNb is explored and the migration energy barrier is determined. Besides, the He clustering behavior in TiZrHfMoNb is investigated. Through transmission electron micros- copy analysis, a smaller He bubble size is observed in TiZrHfMoNb than

in Ti, which is proposed to result from the lower tendency to form He clusters, a weaker coarsening eﬀect, and severe lattice distortion in HEA. The current study thus provides deep insights into the He behaviors in HEAs and may help to develop structural materials with enhanced He damage tolerance in nuclear reactors.

\begin{title}
1. INTRODUCTION
\end{title}

Structural materials used in nuclear reactors must maintain both mechanical properties (strength, ductility, and fracture toughness) and dimensional stability (against creep and void swelling).1−6 Considering the service environment of nuclear reactors,7,8 the insert gas helium (He) produced by (n, α) nuclear transmutation reaction plays a negative role in the macroscopic properties of metals and alloys.9,10 The He atom presents a strong tendency to migrate, aggregate, and form bubbles in an alloy, resulting in surface roughening, blistering, void swelling, and high-temperature intergranular embrittle- ment, which lead to the degradation of the materials’ mechanical strength and shorten the service time.1,11 To ameliorate these problems, the design of structural materials with strong resistance to He damage has been proposed to be a challenging task in the advanced nuclear reactors.

Recently, high-entropy alloys (HEAs), as emerging alloys composed of ﬁve or more metal elements in equal or near equal molar fraction,12 have attracted extensive attention in the ﬁeld of nuclear materials due to their potential He damage tolerance.13,14 Experimentally, Chen et al.15 studied He accumulation and bubble formation at implantation temper- atures of 523, 573, and 673 K in a homogenized HEA FeCoNiCr. They found that the density of He bubbles is

\begin{title}
Metrics & More
\end{title}

temperature-dependent, while the bubble size shows little coarsening eﬀect with the increase of implantation temper- ature. Furthermore, the size of the He bubble is smaller than that in pure Ni and austenitic stainless steel, that is, the HEA FeCoNiCr is more resistant to He bubble growth. Yan et al.16 investigated the behaviors of He in HEA FeCoNiCr at room temperature with diﬀerent He+ ﬂuences and reported that the HEA FeCoNiCr has a higher threshold concentration for He bubble formation relative to the pure Ni and Ni-based equal molar solid solution alloys. Yang et al.17 irradiated the HEA FeCoNiCrMn with a He+ ﬂuence of 2 × 1017 ions/cm2 at both room temperature and 450 °C. As compared with the pure Ni and 304 stainless steel, the HEA FeCoNiCrMn exhibits a smaller He bubble size and better He-ion irradiation resistance. Obviously, the HEAs are more tolerant to He bubble formation than the traditional alloys that are based on one

Received: September 2, 2020 Published: January 21, 2021

Read Online

Article Recommendations

or two principal elements, for example, Fe-, Cr-, or ZrCo-based alloys.18,19

Metal elements in HEAs usually present a sluggish diﬀusion when compared with reference elemental metals.20 Initially, the sluggish diﬀusion eﬀect is just a hypothesis and used to explain the formation of nanosized precipitations in HEAs because the nuclei are relatively easy to form but grow slowly.12 Then, Tsai et al.22 employed a diﬀusion couple approach to investigate the diﬀusion kinetics in HEA. They demonstrated that the diﬀusions of Co, Cr, Fe, Mn, and Ni in CoCrFeMnNi HEA are indeed more sluggish than those in the reference metals. Hence, it is of great interest to investigate the diﬀusion behaviors of He in HEAs. Recently, Zhao et al.23 carried out a ﬁrst-principles calculation to study the growth mechanism of He bubbles in HEA FeCoNiCr. They demonstrated that the dissociation mechanism is dominant in FeCoNiCr at low vacancy concentrations and low He bubble densities are resulted; when vacancy supersaturates, He bubble grows through the vacancy mechanism. In spite of these studies, more detailed and deep insights into the behavior of He in HEAs like site occupation and diﬀusion pathway are still necessary for the potential application of He immobilization in HEAs.

To the best of our knowledge, pure metals such as Ti,24,25 Zr,26 and Mo27,28 are eﬀective in resisting He damage. For HEAs, it has been reported that the physical properties can be adjusted by varying the types of metal elements.18 Hence, the addition of Ti, Zr, and Mo metal elements in HEA may be good for regulating their He damage tolerance. In this work, a systematic study is carried out to investigate the He behaviors in the novel HEA TiZrHfMoNb with a single-phase BCC structure. The formation energy, diﬀusion path, and energy barrier of He in TiZrHfMoNb are obtained and compared to those in Hexagonal Close-Packed (HCP) Ti. An ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) method is further employed to investigate the diﬀusion kinetics of He in TiZrHfMoNb and compared with that in its constituents. In addition, the He bubble morphology in TiZrHfMoNb and HCP Ti are also investigated using the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) technique. The presented results provide deep insights into the behavior of He in HEAs and may help to advance the development of new nuclear material with enhanced He damage tolerance.

\begin{title}
2. COMPUTATIONAL AND EXPERIMENTAL DETAILS
\end{title}

2.1. Computational Details. All the calculations are carried out using the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) code.29 The interaction between ions and electrons is described by the projector- augmented wave (PAW) method.30,31 The exchange and correlation functional are taken in a form proposed by Perdew and Wang (PW91) within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA).32 A number of test calculations have been made and it is shown that a 2 × 2 × 2 k-point sampling in real space and a cutoﬀenergy of 500 eV for the plane wave basis set are necessary for total energies and forces to converge within 1 × 10−4 eV and −1 x 10−3 eV/Å, respectively. In this work, the nudged elastic band (NEB) theory33,34 is used to study the diﬀusion of the He atom in TiZrHfMoNb HEA, which allows one to determine an energy-optimized pathway (reaction path) between two nearest energy minima. To prevent the self-interaction between He atoms during the simulations, a 5 × 5 × 2 TiZrHfMoNb HEA supercell containing 100 atoms is used here, which is illustrated in Figure 1. In our calculations, the lattice constant of TiZrHfMoNb HEA is determined to be 3.365 Å, which is in good agreement with the experimental value of 3.37 Å.35 This ensures the accuracy of the present calculations.

\begin{legend}
OTi  Zr O Hf  Mo  Nb 
\end{legend}

\begin{caption}
Figure 1. Schematic view of the atomic model of the HEA TiZrHfMoNb.
\end{caption}

2.2. Experimental Details. A pure Ti ﬁlm and a TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlm are deposited onto the Mo substrate by magnetron sputtering deposition technique (MSP-300BI) using the Ti and TiZrHfMoNb HEA targets (the HEA targets are synthesized by vacuum arc melting as described previously35), respectively. During the ﬁlm deposition, the mixture gas of an Ar atmosphere with a certain amount of He gas is used as the working gas to prepare the He-containing Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms. Both Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlm depositions are performed under the same conditions to compare the He bubble evolution behaviors. The substrate temperature is set to room temperature, the deposition time is 90 min, the ﬂow ratio between He gas and Ar gas is 20:60 SCCM (standard-state cubic centimeter per minute), and the mixture working pressure is 0.5 Pa. Upon the completion of ﬁlm growth, one set of them are annealed in the quartz tube at 500 °C for 30 min. To avoid severe ﬁlm oxidation, the vacuum pressure is better than 1.0 × 10−4 Pa during annealing. The TEM specimens of the He-containing Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms are prepared by the focused ion beam technique using a Thermo Scientiﬁc Scios 2 workstation. The bright-ﬁeld (BF) images, high- resolution TEM (HRTEM) images, and annual dark-ﬁeld (ADF) image are captured on the cross-sectional He-containing Ti and TiZrHfMoNb specimens using a Thermoﬁsher Scientiﬁc Themis Z spherical aberration TEM working at 300 kV.

\begin{title}
3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS
\end{title}

3.1. Stability of He in HEA TiZrHfMoNb. Before bubble nucleation and growth, He atoms jump from one to another site, and the most important occupation sites for He atoms in metals are interstitial and substitutional sites (vacancies).1 In this subsection, the site preferences of a He atom in octahedral interstitial sites (Heocta), tetrahedral interstitial sites (Hetetra), and substitutional sites (Hesub) are explicitly studied by calculating the formation energy (Ef), which is deﬁned as follows36,37

\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{f}}=E-E_{0}\pm\sum E_{\mathrm{x}}
\end{equation}

\begin{token}
(1)
\end{token}

Here, E and E0 are the total energies of defective and perfect supercells, respectively, and Ex is the reference energy of added (−) or removed (+) atoms x. By this deﬁnition, the lower the formation energy, the much easier the occupation of the He atom. Due to the diﬀerent chemical environments of each occupation site in HEAs, He atoms can have diﬀerent energy states. In order to characterize these energy states, diﬀerent octahedral interstitial sites, tetrahedral interstitial sites, and substitutional sites in HEA TiZrHfMoNb are considered, and the calculated Ef are shown in Figure 2. From a general view, the Ef of He in TiZrHfMoNb are positive, meaning that the incorporation of impurity He atom is an endothermic process, which is similar to the case of He in BCC pure transition metals.10 In addition, it is found that the Ef of Hesub are

\begin{caption}
Figure 2. Formation energies of a single He atom in HEA TiZrHfMoNb at octahedral (octa.), tetrahedral (tetra.), and substitu- tional (sub.) positions. The dashed line denotes the results in Ti.
\end{caption}

generally higher compared with those of Heocta and Hetetra, whereas the diﬀerence in Ef between Heocta and Hetetra is minute, indicating that the interstitial sites are more preferable for He occupation with respect to substitutional sites in HEA TiZrHfMoNb. This case is diﬀerent from that in pure BCC metals, such as Fe, Cr, Mo, and W, where the substitutional site is favorable for He.36

Comparing the Ef for He substituting for diﬀerent metal elements, we ﬁnd that Ti is easier to be substituted due to its lower formation energy. For benchmark purposes, the behaviors of He in HCP Ti are also investigated. The Ef of Heocta, Hetetra, and Hesub in the HCP Ti supercell with 100 atoms are calculated and summarized in Table 1, together with other theoretical results for comparison.38 The diﬀerences in the formation energy between these two studies may be mainly due to the diﬀerences in exchange−correlation functional. In our study and the work of Dai et al.,38 the PW91 and Perdew− Burke−Ernzerhof (PBE) functional are employed, respectively. It can be seen that the Ef of Hetetra is the lowest, and the site preference follows a trend of Hetetra > Heocta > Hesub, which is consistent with other calculation results.38 The Ef for He

\begin{caption}
Table 1. Formation Energies for He Atoms Occupying the Tetrahedral (tetra.), Octahedral (octa.), and Substitutional (sub.) Positions in HCP Ti
\end{caption}

\begin{table}
| occupation sites   | (eV) formation energy   | (eV) formation energy   |
|:-------------------|:------------------------|:------------------------|
| occupation sites   | this work               | Dai et al.38            |
| tetra.             | 2.83                    | 3.00                    |
| octa.              | 2.97                    | 3.19                    |
| sub.               | 3.61                    | 3.65                    |
\end{table}

occupying diﬀerent sites in Ti are indicated by the dotted lines in Figure 2. It is shown that the Ef values for Heocta, Hetetra, and Hesub in HEA TiZrHfMoNb are distributed widely with respect to that in Ti. Obviously, a wide distribution of Ef may result in larger formation energy diﬀerence between two adjacent occupation sites. Furthermore, the Ef of He in TiZrHfMoNb at octahedral, tetrahedral, and substitutional sites are generally lower than those in Ti, meaning that smaller energies are needed for He occupation in TiZrHfMoNb. To investigate the He clustering behavior in TiZrHfMoNb and Ti, we further calculate the formation energies of a He cluster containing two and three He atoms. The results are presented in Figure 3a,b. It is shown that the formation energies of a He cluster in TiZrHfMoNb are generally higher than those in Ti, that is, more energies are needed for He clustering in TiZrHfMoNb. This result suggests that He clustering is more diﬃcult in TiZrHfMoNb compared with Ti.

3.2. Diﬀusion of He Atoms in HEA TiZrHfMoNb. Besides the He occupation behavior, we also investigate the He atom diﬀusion in HEA TiZrHfMoNb. Considering that the interstitial sites are energetically more favorable with respect to the substitutional sites for He occupation in HEA TiZrHf- MoNb, the diﬀusion of a single He atom between interstitial sites is investigated by an NEB method. Referring to the calculated Ef in Section 3.1, the most stable interstitial site for He occupation is set as the initial position (I-site). Furthermore, three other stable tetrahedral interstitial sites, that is, F1-site, F2-site, and F3-site, as indicated in Figure 4a, are selected as the ﬁnal positions for He diﬀusion. Five possible diﬀusion paths are considered, namely, path 1, path 2, path3, path 4, and path 5 (as presented in Figure 4a), where path 1− 2, path 3−4, and path 5 correspond to He diﬀusion along the I →F1, I →F2, and I →F3 paths, respectively. The diﬀusion pathways and related energy proﬁles for paths 1−5 are illustrated in Figure 4b−f. It can be seen that the diﬀusion barriers setting oﬀfrom the I-site are 0.27 and 0.03 eV for path 1, 0.3 and 0.01 eV for path 2, 0.56 and 0.52 eV for path 3, 0.96 and 0.13 eV for path 4, and 0.27, 0.76 and 0.37 eV for path 5, which indicate that the distribution of barrier values in HEA is not uniform. Such a low energy barrier of a He atom in alloys indicates that the He atom can diﬀuse generally fast even below room temperature, which has been observed in other pure metals and alloys, such as W,39,40 Fe,9 and NiCoFeCr.23 The maximum energy barriers of path 1, path 2, path 3, path 4, and path 5 are 0.27, 0.3, 0.56, 0.76, and 0.96 eV, respectively, presenting a very remarkable anisotropic feature. Comparing the energy barriers for He diﬀusion along diﬀerent paths, we ﬁnd that path 1 and path 2 are more favorable due to the relatively lower energy barriers.

For comparison, the diﬀusion of He in pure metal Ti from a tetrahedral interstitial site to its ﬁrst neighboring tetrahedral interstitial site is also investigated by the NEB method. The migration path of He and the corresponding energy barrier are

\begin{legend}
(a)
\end{legend}

\begin{caption}
Figure 3. Formation energies of the He cluster containing (a) two and (b) three He atoms in HEA TiZrHfMoNb. The dashed line denotes the results in Ti.
\end{caption}

given in Figure 5a,b, respectively. The energy barrier of this reaction is determined to be 0.24 eV, which is lower than that in HEA TiZrHfMoNb (0.27−0.96 eV). Lu et al.19 demon- strated that short-range three-dimensional motions of atoms can signiﬁcantly increase the diﬀusion energy barrier in the alloys as compared with one-dimensional motions. The concept of one-dimensional motion is that the atom migrates along one direction in the lattice, meaning that the system must retain a high symmetry. In this work, the diﬀusion path of Ti presents a character of high-symmetry, and the saddle point is explicitly located in the middle of the pathway (Figure 5), which exhibits the common feature in many high-symmetry conﬁgurations.41 In contrast, the trajectories for paths 1−5 in TiZrHfMoNb HEA are diverse, and the corresponding energies along these paths exhibit great ﬂuctuation with no symmetry (Figure 4). Consequently, the decreased symmetry of HEA TiZrHfMoNb makes the He atom prone to undergo a short-range three-dimensional motion relative to Ti, thus resulting in a higher energy barrier.

To further explore the He diﬀusion behavior in HEAs, a set of AIMD simulations are carried out to investigate the displacement of a He atom in TiZrHfMoNb and its constituents (Ti, Zr, Hf, Mo, and Nb) for 6 ps. It is worth noting that the He displacement obtained by AIMD simulation is thermally driven, which is diﬀerent from the He atomic displacement caused by collision with incident radiation (e.g., neutrons and ions). Experimentally, Zhang et al.42 conducted He implantations in concentrated solid-solution alloys at 773 K to investigate He bubble formation. Hence, in this study, the temperature of 800 K is chosen for AIMD simulations. Figure 6a,b plots the variation of energy and temperature with time for He-containing TiZrHfMoNb, respectively. It is shown that the total energy of the TiZrHfMoNb HEA at a temperature of 800 K vibrates slightly with time evolution, meaning that the system reaches an equilibrium state. As shown in Figure 7, the average displacements of He in TiZrHfMoNb, Ti, Zr, Hf, Mo, and Nb are calculated to be 0.6, 0.78, 0.77, 3.76, 2.42, and 13.92 Å, respectively. Zr presents the smallest He displacement among the pure metals (Ti, Zr, Hf, Mo, and Nb). Meanwhile, the largest He displacement is observed in Nb due to an extremely low energy barrier (0.008 eV) for He migration.43 By contrast, HEA TiZrHfMoNb has the smallest He displacement of 0.6 Å compared with the case of He in its

\begin{legend}
(b)
\end{legend}

constituents (Figure 7), agreeing well with the results of NEB calculations.

To explore the relationship between the compositional complexity and He diﬀusion in HEAs, ﬁve ideal BCC models consisting of diﬀerent metal elements (Ti, TiZr, TiZrHf, TiZrHfMo, and TiZrHfMoNb) are considered in the subsequent AIMD calculations. In order to remove the eﬀect of lattice distortion, the shape of the supercell and atomic positions of the metal elements are ﬁxed while the He atom is allowed to move during the AIMD calculations. Figure 8 presents the average displacement of He as a function of the number of the metal elements in those ideal models. It can be seen that with the increasing number of metal elements, the average displacement of He is decreasing, meaning that tuning the compositional complexity in traditional metals can signiﬁcantly retard the diﬀusion of He.

3.3. He Bubble Growth in TiZrHfMoNb and Ti Films. A detailed TEM analysis is conducted to investigate the He bubble morphology in the He-containing TiZrHfMoNb and Ti specimens. The magnetron sputtering deposition technique is employed to introduce He in HEA and Ti by which the He can be uniformly distributed in the metal ﬁlm and the ﬁlm damage can be eﬀectively prevented.45 During the ﬁlm deposition, helium gas and the thin ﬁlms are deposited at the same time. Figure 9 shows the TEM bright-ﬁeld images at diﬀerent depths (P1, P2, and P3) in an annealed Ti ﬁlm. It is clear that the helium bubble is uniformly distributed at diﬀerent positions. The BF images and the HRTEM images of He-containing Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms are presented in Figure 10. The thicknesses of the He-containing Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms are 2.21 ± 0.05 and 2.73 ± 0.05 μm, respectively. As shown in Figure 10a,c, both Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms present a columnar grain structure feature with the grain width ranging from 0.1 to 0.4 μm. As the He is homogenously distributed in the ﬁlms, the central region of Ti and TiZrHfMoNb ﬁlms are selected for analysis. The rectangular areas denoted in Figure 10a,c are magniﬁed and shown in Figure 10b,d, respectively. It is clear that a great number of white quasi-circular spots appear in the HRTEM images. The major reason for the presence of white spots is the formation of He bubbles as the He gas is ionized and deposited together with the sputtered metal atoms. Considering the low damage of HEA and Ti ﬁlms, the concentration of vacancy defects in Ti and TiZrHfMoNb

