\begin{title}
Synthesis and Phase Stability of the High-Entropy Carbide (Ti0.2Zr0.2Nb0.2Ta0.2Mo0.2)C under Extreme Conditions
\end{title}

Shixue Guan, Hao Liang, Qiming Wang, Lijie Tan, and Fang Peng*

Cite This: Inorg. Chem. 2021, 60, 3807−3813

\begin{title}
ACCESS
\end{title}

\begin{title}
ACCESS Metrics & More
\end{title}

ABSTRACT: As a novel ultrahigh temperature ceramic, the stability of a high-entropy transition metal carbide under extreme conditions is of great concern to its application. Despite the intense research, the available high-pressure experimental results are few so far. Here, we synthesized the nanocrystalline (Ti0.2Zr0.2Nb0.2Ta0.2Mo0.2)C by a high- pressure solid-state reaction successfully. Meanwhile, synchrotron radiation X-ray diﬀraction experiments were carried out to explore the phase stability and mechanical response under high pressure. The single cubic B1 phase structure of the high-entropy carbide is retained under extreme hydrostatic pressure. An abnormal cubic-to-cubic phase transition was observed unexpectedly under nonhydrostatic compression. This result reﬂects the eﬀect of the severe lattice distortion of the initial B1 phase high- entropy carbide and the shear strain caused by deviatoric stress under high nonhydrostatic pressure. The physical mechanism about electronic/magnetic character- istics behind ﬁndings is an interesting issue for future studies.

\begin{title}
INTRODUCTION
\end{title}

From the Stone Age to the present, the rapid development of science and technology depends on the properties of the available materials. When the development of traditional materials cannot meet the rapidly evolving needs of new technologies, it becomes particularly important to quest late- model means for advanced materials design. Cantor et al. and Yeh et al. discovered an exciting new concept of multiprincipal element alloys (MPEAs) or high-entropy alloys (HEAs) in 2004, which can be deﬁned as solid solutions commonly with ﬁve or more equal or near-equal molar elements.1,2 The maximized conﬁgurational entropy in the system stabilizes a single-phase solid solution by increasing the composition element.3−5 In highly disordered, multicomponent systems, high-entropy alloys have properties that traditional alloys cannot realize, including well-structural stability and excellent mechanical properties (high ductility and strength as well as excellent corrosion and wear resistance).2,4−6

With the deepening of research, the concept of high entropy expands to other materials.7 High-entropy transition metal carbides (HETMCs) are also drawing attention in the recent research articles as a kind of advanced ceramic materials.8−13 HETMCs were generally composed of ﬁve or more transition metals and the C element, where the C element occupies the anion position and metal elements share a cation position. HETMCs, similar to the unexpected properties of HEAs, also have high-entropy eﬀects, sluggish kinetics, lattice distortions, and synergetic eﬀects.2,14

As binary carbides, HETMCs also display favorable melting point, electricity, mechanism, and chemistry capability due to their strong covalent bond.15 They are the suitable materials

Read Online

Article Recommendations

that are available to make or protect components in extreme operating environments such as nuclear reactors, aeroengines, and hypersonic vehicles. Thus, it is necessary to research on the stability and mechanical properties of HETMC materials under extreme conditions to optimize the design and manufacturing of components in extreme operating environ- ments. The Gibbs free energy is signiﬁcantly reduced in the system, as HETMCs possess maximized conﬁgurational entropy;3 moreover, the Gibbs free energy (G = PV −TS) can be expected to be aﬀected by pressure and temperature tuning. The stability of HETMCs as a function of pressure and temperature contributes to give an insight into these late- model materials and also explore their potential applications. But the full understanding of how temperature or/and pressure (extreme conditions) aﬀect the HETMC performance have not been explored experimentally before. High pressures as one of the basic parameters besides temperature can change the interatomic distance or even bonding patterns and electronic conﬁguration to control the material structure and performance.16−18 Lately, it was found that HEAs and high- entropy oxides (HEOs) with seemingly ultrastable structures demonstrate rich polymorphism at room temperature and high

Received: December 7, 2020

Published: February 22, 2021

Supporting Information

Fiveindividual metal carbides

pressure.19−24 So, what are the structures and properties in HETMCs under high pressure?

In this paper, a high-temperature and high-pressure (HTHP) process of equimolar quinary metallic carbides to one-step direct synthesis was carried out with a single-phase nanocrystalline high-entropy transition metal carbide (Ti0.2Zr0.2Nb0.2Ta0.2Mo0.2)C ((Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C will be used hereafter for simplicity) with a relatively low temperature of 1600 °C. Then, in situ synchrotron radiation X-ray diﬀraction (XRD) techniques were executed to investigate the structural evolution and mechanical response of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C. It was found that both the bulk modulus and yield strength of the HETMC are universally superior to the average properties of the corresponding ﬁve individual transition metal carbides. In a nonhydrostatic environment, we discovered that the starting material of the single-phase structure showed abnormal high-pressure phase evolution as a result of the severe lattice distortion and inhomogeneous stress. The ﬁndings of our work are valuable and oﬀer basic knowledge for the optimization design and applications of novel HETMCs in years to come.

\begin{title}
SECTION
\end{title}

Sample Synthesis at High Pressure and High Temperature. TiC, ZrC, NbC, TaC, and Mo2C (Alfa Aesar, >99% purity, 2−5 μm) powders were used as reaction precursors for the HPHT synthesis of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C at 5.5 GPa and temperatures ranging from 1500 to 2000 °C. The experiments of the high-entropy ceramic material synthesis were carried out on a large volume press (DS6 × 14MN, China) at a high temperature and high pressure. Prior to HPHT synthesis experiments, equimolar amounts of ﬁve precursors, on a metal basis, were high-energy ball milled (HEBM) using tungsten carbide milling media in a high-purity argon atmosphere for 50 h. To minimize overheating and oxidation, we performed the milling process for 10 h segments with a 1 h cool-oﬀperiod. Then, the reaction precursors mixed by HEBM were wrapped by an hBN column and placed in a graphite sleeve for heating the samples in HPHT experiments.

Characterization. A DX-2700 BH diﬀractometer with Cu Kα radiation (λ = 1.5404 Å) was used to execute X-ray diﬀraction (XRD) to identify phase compositions. Raman characterizations were carried out in a custom-built Raman system (Andor Shamrock SR-303i-B, Andor Newton DU970P-UVB, EU; RGB laser system, GER). A scanning electron microscope (SEM, JSM-IT500HR, JPN) equipped with an energy-dispersive spectroscope (EDS) was used to analyze the microstructure, particle size, and compositional uniformity.

In Situ High-Pressure Synchrotron Radiation Information Acquisition. At the Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility (BSRF, China) 4W2 beamline, in situ high-pressure XRD experiments on HETMCs were conducted. The incident monochromatic X-ray beam with a wavelength of 0.6199 Å was focused by a pair of Kirkpatrick− Baez mirrors to a spot. High pressure was generated by a symmetrical diamond anvil cell (DAC) with a pair of 300 μm culets. We have performed experiments for both hydrostatic and nonhydrostatic environments of HETMCs. Ruby ball ﬂuorescence shifts and the equation of state (EOS) for the samples served as a pressure standard.

\begin{title}
AND DISCUSSION
\end{title}

High-Entropy Carbide Solid Solution Phase Forma- tion. Five kinds of commercial metallic carbide precursors of equimolar amounts were mixed for mechanical alloying by the HEBM method to synthesize the high-entropy metal carbide (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C. The SEM images and XRD patterns of the initial mixed and HEBM powders are shown in Figure S1. The HEBM powders have a smaller grain size (0.08−2 μm) with a few micrograins and lots of nanograins, as shown in

Figure S1b. Also, in Figure S1c, the decreased grain size broadens the diﬀraction proﬁle. Subsequently, we carried out HPHT experiments using the HEBM powders at 5.5 GPa and 1500−1800 °C. The crystalline structure of each sample is determined by XRD and Raman scattering technique, whereas the chemical homogeneity is detected using EDS. The evolution and formation of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C are shown in Figure 1a at diﬀerent temperatures, demonstrating that the

\begin{caption}
