electronic orbital and the bonding pattern. It can reverse the sign of the free energy diﬀerence and reduce the energy barrier to more easily incorporate conﬁgurational entropy to form a high-entropy metal carbide of a single-phase solid solution.17 So, the high-pressure solid-state reaction method is a promising technique, which opens an additional avenue to prepare an HETMC. Figure 1c shows the Raman spectrum of four individual metal carbides (Mo2C no detected Raman peaks) and the high-entropy carbide (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C. The Raman peaks of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C are dominated by broad features centered at ∼95, ∼111, ∼271, ∼333, ∼650, and ∼971 cm−1. Its Raman peaks are not the superposition of individual carbides but its own unique Raman peaks, which are similar to NbC (∼119, ∼266, ∼645, and ∼990 cm−1). It indicates that (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C has a vibration mode similar to NbC. From this result, we can infer that NbC may be inclined to play the part of the host material; other metal atoms randomly dissolved into a NbC lattice to form a single-solid solution, which makes conﬁgurational entropy reach the maximum, thereby stabilizing the high-entropy system. The weakening of the Raman peak can be understood as the severe local lattice distortion in (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C caused by the chemical disorder.21 In Figure 1d, a typical SEM image of the recovered sample is shown to gain insights into the microstructural features. The (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C specimens obtained by HPHT consist of very ﬁne grains of nanometer size (∼260 nm). Compared with initial HEBM powders, the (Ti,Zr,Nb,- Ta,Mo)C specimens have a more uniform grain size and smaller grain size. This is the result of sluggish kinetics and the pressure synergistic eﬀect. The high pressure should dominate the formation of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C nanocrystals. Under high pressure, the particles fracture completely or partially when the local high shear stress reaches the strength of the individual grains.26 Meanwhile, compared with the conventional method27 for preparing high-entropy ceramics, the grain growth is inhibited under pressure to form nanosized grain samples.

Mechanical Response of High-Entropy Carbides. The mechanical and structural stabilities of high-entropy carbides are crucial for their applications under extreme conditions. A DAC experiment is used to achieve an extreme stress environment that squeezes materials to generate hydrostatic/ nonhydrostatic pressure. In Figure S3, the pressure evolutions under the hydrostatic environment of (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C (in the silicone oil medium) are depicted. It can be observed that no phase transitions occurred in (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C at pressures up to ∼50 GPa, which suggests that the single- cubic-phase structure of the high-entropy carbide is excep- tionally retained under extreme pressure. With the increase of pressure, diﬀraction peaks of as-synthesized (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo) C move toward a larger diﬀraction angle, which reﬂects pressure-induced tighter packing of the atoms directly. On account of increasing strain inhomogeneity and microstrains, the reﬂections present progressive broadening. As shown in Figure 2a, the Birch−Murnaghan equation of state (BM-EOS) is used to ﬁt the normalized volume as a function of pressure, with a unit cell volume of 85.16 Å3, an isothermal bulk modulus (K) of 311(4) GPa, and the corresponding pressure derivative (K′) is 5.5(0.3) or K = 332(2) GPa and K′ is ﬁxed at 4 for (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C. As shown in Figure 2b, the precision of the EOS ﬁtting results can be measured based on the normalized stress against the Eulerian deﬁnition of ﬁnite strain and so the results are accurate. Its bulk modulus is

