II into BM-EOS with a ﬁxed bulk modulus pressure derivative K′ = 4 yields a bulk modulus K of 408(18) GPa and a volume V0 of 84.86 Å3 are observed. Due to pseudo-incompressibility caused by the nonhydrostatic environment, the data of phase II are invalid for compression behavior and state equation analysis.38 However, we still can observe an obvious isostructural transition with volume collapse and a 1.5% volume change from phase I to phase II at ∼9.8 GPa. In this work, the cubic-to-cubic isostructural transition phenomenon of HETMC was not found under the hydrostatic environment but in the nonhydrostatic environment. Why thus pressure environment diﬀerences cause a phase transition?

There are many kinds of elements in the high-entropy carbide, which exist in the same molar ratio, and the atoms of each element can occupy the positions of each lattice with the same opportunity. Due to the diﬀerent sizes of atoms, severe lattice distortion can be caused. It is to be expected that if the diﬀerence in the atomic size is large enough, the distorted lattice can collapse into an amorphous phase because the lattice distortion is too high to maintain the crystal conﬁguration. There are some diﬀerences in the atom radii (Ti: 1.70 Å, Zr: 2.06 Å, Nb: 1.98 Å, Ta: 2.0 Å, and Mo: 1.9 Å) of the solid dissolved atoms in the (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C HETMC, which result in the crystal conﬁguration being severely distorted (see the inset of Figure 5, phase I). In comparison with the high-pressure isostructural electronic transition that appears in Ce and SmS,39,40 this transition seems to be related to the change in the electronic state. In the case of the (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C HETMC, at a chemically complex high-entropy system, there are diﬀerent ionic charges, radii, electron shell structure elements, and disordered electron orbital interactions. There is no phase transition under an

extreme hydrostatic pressure of ∼50.2 GPa, indicating that it seems impossible to produce an electron transition under pressure. Therefore, the pressure-induced cubic-to-cubic transition of the (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C HETMC seems improb- able due to the transition between these states of electrons under nonhydrostatic pressure. Why do isostructural phase transitions exist only in the nonhydrostatic environment? It can be explained by the diﬀerence between the two stressful environments. For hydrostatic pressure conditions, because of the existence of a pressure-transmitting medium, it can be considered that under high pressure, the sample is in an environment with uniform forces in all directions. But for the nonhydrostatic pressure environment with no pressure-trans- mitting medium, in the uniaxial compressional stress provided by DAC, the diﬀerential stress between the maximum and minimum compression directions provides nonuniform stress in all directions. It is because of the stress diversiﬁcation and anisotropy of the (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C HETMC caused by severe lattice distortion, which prompts phase I to collapse into isostructural phase II sharply at low pressure. This is a ﬁrst- order phase transition. This phase transition is a process of the nonuniform stress-normalized crystal structure (phase I to phase II) and this normalization process usually occurs in the lower pressure range where usually large continuous changes in the volume are observed. The (Ti,Zr,Nb,Ta,Mo)C HETMC with serious lattice distortion has a discontinuous volume change, which results in a cubic-to-cubic phase isostructural transition.

\begin{title}
CONCLUSIONS
\end{title}

In summary, we obtain a nanoscale high-entropy carbide (Ti,Zr,Nb,Mo,Ta)C via the high-temperature and high- pressure synthesis; meanwhile, it is also shown to be stable in extreme operating environments. The atomic structural evolution and mechanical response of the ordered fcc- structured (B1 phase) (Ti,Zr,Nb,Mo,Ta)C HETMC were studied during hydrostatic and nonhydrostatic environment compressions of up to 50.2 and 34.8 GPa using in situ synchrotron radiation XRD in DAC. The pressure−volume experimental data ﬁtted to the third- and second-order Birch− Murnaghan equations of state indicated the bulk moduli are K0 = 311(4) GPa and 332(2) GPa, respectively, which are greater than the averages of the bulk moduli from individual metal carbides (∼20 and 30%). We observed the cubic-to-cubic isostructural phase transition of high-entropy phase (Ti,Zr,Nb,- Mo,Ta)C and the obvious isostructural transition with volume collapse and a 1.5% volume change from phase I to phase II under ∼9.8 GPa. We discussed the cubic-to-cubic isostructural transition mechanism. It is closely associated with the severely distorted crystal conﬁguration of the normalized crystal structure by the nonuniform stress under low nonhydrostatic pressure. Pressure could be applied as a potent tool to synthesize novel HETMCs, which are not easily available in other methods, and also to understand the mechanical and physical properties of HETMCs to make them attractive candidates for technological applications under extreme conditions.

