\begin{legend}
Hg
\end{legend}

\begin{caption}
Figure 1. (A) TEM image of the AuHgPt nanoalloy, (B) HRTEM image of the AuHgPt nanoalloy and its corresponding lattice spacing and mismatch (red border), (C) HAADF-STEM image and corresponding elemental mappings of the AuHgPt nanoalloy.
\end{caption}

Rossmeisl and co-workers synthesized Pt−Hg and Pd−Hg nanoparticles for the electrochemical synthesis of H2O2. The Pd−Hg nanoparticles not only adjust the overpotential and HOO* binding energy to have high catalytic activity but also exhibit at least some stability against dissolution.12,13 Due to the superior alloying/dealloying ability and high ﬂexibility of amalgam ﬁlms, lithium−mercury (Li−Hg) hybrid anodes were easy to prepare and exhibit a long life in batteries, and the amalgam protection can greatly inhibit the dendrite of Li metal batteries.14 In the ﬁeld of environmental analysis, the selective detection and removal of mercury ions in polluted water can be realized by using the strong bonding force between Hg and Au nanoparticles.15,16 More importantly, electro-oxidation treat- ment is an eﬀective method to recover and reuse Hg from Hg- containing materials, which can be used to reduce the harm of mercury to the environment and humans.17 Based on the above considerations, an exploratory strategy to replace Cu or Ni in the heterostructures with Hg on the preparation of noble metal multialloys by the GRR is proposed.

Apart from the basic role of composition, the catalytic activity is also aﬀected by structural morphology, such as interatomic distance, band gap, the number of adjacent metal atoms, and surface metal content.18,19 In various morphologies, the surface atoms play the main role in enhancing the electrocatalytic activity, while the use of atoms inside the nanoparticles is still limited.20,21 It is necessary to design a nanoalloy with a large surface area and defective atoms and expose numbers of active sites for catalysts. A hollow nanoalloy is a promising strategy that can maximize the use of edges and internal atoms and expose as many active sites as possible, which is concerned in the ﬁeld of electrocatalysis.22−25 Until now, various kinds of noble metal-based hollow-structured nanomaterials have been fabricated.26−28 Among these structures, hollow gold-based nanoalloys are considered as interesting components in the catalyst attributed to the advantages of simple synthesis, easy operation, and good stability. Furthermore, the introduction of gold (Au) can not only promote the oxidation of intermediates29 but also change the electronic structure of the nanoalloys [such as gold− platinum (AuPt) and gold−palladium (AuPd)], reducing the corresponding d-band center, which leads to an enhancement in catalyst activity and stability.30,31

In this work, a hollow Au nanoparticle was synthesized as a template for the preparation of hollow multimetallic nanoalloys by the GRR using Hg as an intermediate under mild

experimental conditions. The hollow gold-based nanoparticle can easily form amalgams, and the Hg in the nanoalloys was replaced with Pt and/or Pd atoms to synthesize Pt- or Pd- loaded ternary (AuHgPt or AuHgPd) or even quaternary (AuHgPtPd or AuHgPdPt) hollow nanoalloys. Employing high-angle annular dark-ﬁeld scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), X-ray photoelectron spectros- copy (XPS), and X-ray diﬀraction (XRD), we have conﬁrmed that this hollow nanostructure has the characteristics of the nanoalloys. Besides, linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) and chronopotentiometry were used to assess the ability of the prepared nanoalloys to catalyze hydrogen evolution. The results showed that for the alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), the Pt-exposed nanoalloys exhibited more eﬀective electrocatalytic performance with the smaller over- potential and Tafel slope among a series of synthesized catalysts.

\begin{title}
2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
\end{title}

2.1. Characterizations of the Gold-Based Nanoalloys. Figure S1 shows the TEM images of the uniformly distributed hollow Au nanoparticles, and the hollow AuHg nanoalloys underwent moderate agglomeration due to the inﬂuence of Hg.32 Pt(IV)/Pt(0) and Pd(II)/Pd(0) having higher reduction potential than that of Hg(II)/Hg(0) are likely to sponta- neously replace Hg atoms. The Pt and/or Pd atoms were introduced by the galvanic displacement between the Hg species of the amalgams and Pt(IV) and Pd(II) in the aqueous solutions to synthesize the ternary (AuHgPt and AuHgPd) and quaternary (AuHgPtPd and AuHgPdPt) hollow nanoalloys. Figure 1A displays the particle morphology of the AuHgPt nanoalloy prepared by the GRR, and the high-resolution TEM image (HRTEM) reveals the AuHgPt nanoalloy with a well- deﬁned lattice plane (Figure 1B). At the same time, some small nanoparticles that can be identiﬁed as Hg2Cl2 are around the nanoalloys.33 Metallic alloys may alter the electronic structure of the metal species, resulting in a change in lattice spacing. The interplanar spacing of the AuPt alloy (0.231 nm) is diﬀerent from those of (111)Au (2.35 Å) and (111)Pt (2.25 Å), which is indicative of AuPt alloy formation.34,35 The fringe lattice for one direction was calculated around 0.250 nm, which is also evidence of the alloying of Au and Pt.36 The results are consistent with the previous literature, demonstrat- ing the formation of a bimetallic AuPt alloy.37 Taking the

\begin{legend}
Pt
\end{legend}

\begin{legend}
Pd
\end{legend}

\begin{legend}
Hg
\end{legend}

\begin{caption}
Figure 2. Low-resolution (A) and HR (B) TEM images of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy. (C) STEM-HAADF images of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy and the corresponding EDS elemental mapping results.
\end{caption}

\begin{caption}
Figure 3. XPS spectra of Au 4f, Pt 4f, and Pd 3d of the AuHgPt (A) and the AuHgPdPt (B) nanoalloys.
\end{caption}

AuHgPt nanoalloy as an example for detailed analysis, the spatial distribution of Au, Hg, and Pt elements in the multimetallic nanoalloy was examined using a HAADF- STEM with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). As illustrated in Figure 1C, the mapping images show that Au and Pt are uniformly distributed throughout the hollow nanostruc- ture but less elemental Hg, proving the existence of the alloy structure. Besides, the atomic molar ratio of Au, Hg, and Pt in the ternary AuHgPt nanoalloy was roughly determined by EDS to be around 10.1:0.3:89.6. TEM images of the AuHgPd hollow nanoalloy (Figure S2) show that the lattice spacings corresponding to the (111) plane of Au and Pd were determined to be 0.235 and 0.214 nm, respectively.38 According to the recorded lattice spacings, 0.235 nm (Au(111) plane) > 0.217 nm > 0.214 nm (Pd(111) plane) and 0.206 nm (Au(200) plane) > 0.198 nm > 0.196 nm (Pd(200) plane crystal grid spacing), the measured interplanar spacing of AuHgPd (0.217 and 0.198 nm) is lower than the interplanar spacing of the Au metal and higher than the interplanar spacing of Pd, demonstrating the incorporation of Pd into the Au lattices.39−41 It was proved that the ternary nanoalloys were formed by the GRR under mild experimental conditions (room temperature). It should also be noted that, due to the diﬀerence in lattice constant between Au and Pd/Pt, while Pd/Pt exchanged into the AuHg nanoalloys by the displacement reaction, elastic stress is generated at the interface of the AuHg alloy, causing crystal defects or lattice mismatch (inset of the red border).

With the ternary nanoalloys acting as hollow nanoparticle templates, the galvanic replacement strategy can also be

extended to produce multiple noble metallic nanoalloys, where Hg acts as a replacement medium. The ternary nanoalloys recorporated with Hg as precursors were incubated into the noble metal ion solutions containing diﬀerent compositions (Pt(IV) and Pd(II)) and subjected to the GRR to yield quaternary (AuHgPdPt and AuHgPtPd) hollow nanoalloys. Figure 2A is a typical TEM image of the hollow AuHgPdPt nanoparticles and some small nanoparticles are observed around the Au−Hg hollow structure, which could be accounted for Hg2Cl2.33 The HRTEM image of a AuHgPdPt nanoalloy (Figure 2B) shows a clear lattice fringe, and the interval distance between adjacent lattice stripes is 0.250 nm, accounting for the AuPt alloy.42,43 A lattice fringe of the (200) plane with a d-spacing constant of 0.201 nm can be assigned to the AuPd alloy, which is higher than the lattice fringe of face- centered cubic (fcc) Pd (0.196 nm) but slightly lower than that of Au (0.206 nm).35,44 Besides, an emerging lattice spacing of 0.224 nm is adjacent to the (111) and (200) planes of the fcc PtPd alloy.45,46 The alloying structure of the quaternary AuHgPdPt nanoparticle can also be certiﬁed by EDS elemental mapping with HAADF-STEM. The EDS elemental mapping reveals that the hollow nanostructure is made of an alloy, with Au, Pd, and Pt being evenly distributed (Figure 2C), and the HAADF-STEM images of the similar brightness can further conﬁrm the nanoalloy rather than a core−shell structure. The TEM images of the AuHgPtPd nanoalloy obtained by the same method are brieﬂy introduced in Figure S3. The lattice spacing of 0.217 nm is contributed by the presence of the AuPd nanoalloy,47 and the lattice fringes with d-spacing constants of 0.201 and 0.250 nm correspond to AuPd and AuPt alloys,

\begin{caption}
Figure 4. (A) HER polarization curve of the prepared nanoalloys and Pt/C in 1.0 mol L−1 KOH solution with a scan rate of 1 mV s−1, (B) corresponding Tafel curves derived from (A). Exchange current density (j0) extrapolated from the Tafel plots (C) and the chronoamperometric analysis of diﬀerent catalysts (D) in 1.0 mol L−1 KOH solution with a potential of −0.2 V.
\end{caption}

respectively.48 In addition, the hollow multimetallic nanoalloy has a rough surface, indicating the presence of a large number of uncoordinated sites that tend to promote catalysis.49

In order to further analyze the composition of the multimetallic nanoalloys, the synthesized nanoparticles were measured by XPS. Figures 3 and S4 show typical XPS spectra of the Au, Hg, Pt, and Pd regions of the nanoalloys. As Figure 3A shows, two peaks assigned to Au 4f7/2 and Au 4f5/2 of the ternary AuHgPt nanoalloy arise at 84.2 and 87.9 eV, respectively.50 The study of Pt 4f energy testiﬁes that Pt existed in the form of metal and oxide states. The binding energies of Pt 4f5/2 and Pt 4f7/2 positively shifted to 74.1 and 71.0 eV, which conﬁrmed that the electronic interaction occurred between the atomic orbitals of the nanoalloy caused by the shifting of electrons.51,52 Similar results can also be found in the XPS investigation of the AuHgPd nanoalloy (Figure S4A). The existence of Pd(II) observed as the main peaks located at 337.8 and 343.0 eV cannot be ignored. This could be due to the easy oxidation of the exposed Pd in the environmental system.53−55 Based on the XPS spectrum of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy shown in Figure 3B, Hg, Au, Pt, and Pd were presented. Compared with the AuHgPd nanoalloy, the metallic Pd occupies the main component, which may be due to the fact that the later exchanged Pt inhibited the oxidation of the inner Pd atoms (Figure 3B). The Pt 4f spectrum is deconvolved into ﬁve peaks. The peaks at 74.7 and 70.9 eV are assigned to Pt 4f5/2 and Pt 4f7/2, while the peaks at 72.9, 76.2 and 78.3 eV could be an evidence for the partial surface oxidation of Pt,56,57 and the peak appearing at 333.0 eV is attributed to Pt 4d3/2.58 The peak intensity of Pt 4d3/2 is signiﬁcantly weakened, and the metallic Pt can be the main species in the AuHgPtPd nanoalloy (Figure S4B), suggesting the entire reduction of Pt(IV) and the protection of the Pd layers. From the results of XPS, it is conﬁrmed that the ternary and quaternary nanoalloys were successfully prepared by means of the GRRs between metals.

The XRD proﬁle of the above series of nanoalloys shows the crystal structure of the metal fcc (Figure S5). The diﬀraction peaks at 38.06 and 44.2° are assigned to the planes of Au (111)

and (200),59 and the peak at 32.04° can be indexed to the (200) plane of AgCl,60 a byproduct of the synthesis of hollow Au nanoparticles. After synthesizing gold amalgam, the diﬀraction peak of 32.9° belongs to mercuric oxide.61 For Pt and/or Pd exposed on the nanoalloys, the characteristic diﬀraction peaks of the two elements ((200) crystal faces) can be observed simultaneously with the positions of Au(111) in the nanoalloys that shifted toward higher diﬀraction. These results demonstrate how Pt or Pd expands the interatomic distance of Au−Au.36 Besides, three extra diﬀraction peaks can be observed in Pt-/Pd-based nanoalloys. The peaks at 2θ = 20.46, 28.24, and 32.34° are assigned to the planes of Hg2Cl2 (101), (110), and (004), respectively.33,62 These results veriﬁed that the formation of multialloys based on the replacement of Hg by the GRRs can provide a new strategy for the synthesis of multiple noble metallic nanoalloys at room temperature and make it possible to rationally select the catalysts for particular heterogeneous chemical reactions.

2.2. Electrocatalytic HER Performance with Diﬀerent Nanoalloys. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is a powerful technique for characterizing interfacial reactions and electrode kinetics in the HER. Rapid electron transfer is one of the key factors contributing to HER kinetics. Figure S6 shows representative Nyquist plots of the EIS responses for diﬀerent gold-based nanoalloys with an overpotential of −0.2 V [vs reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)] in 1.0 mol L−1 KOH solution. In the high-frequency region, ﬂattened semicircles are displayed, indicating that the HERs are kinetically controlled.44 The semicircle corresponds to the charge transfer resistance Rct and represents the electron transfer rate; a smaller semicircle can be expressed as a faster reaction rate.63,64 In this system, the semicircles decrease signiﬁcantly with increasing alloy type; among the nanoalloys, the charge transfer resistance of the AuHgPdPt has a smaller circularity and is much lower than the other nanomaterials, which demonstrates that the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy has good charge transfer characteristics and excellent HER performance.

Figure 4A presents the HER polarization curves with iR correction in 1.0 mol L−1 KOH at room temperature by LSV at

a scan rate of 1 mV s−1. The AuHgPdPt nanoalloy has a low onset overpotential (deﬁned as an overpotential at a current density of 1.0 mA cm−2) of 12 mV. At a current density of 10 mA cm−2, the overpotential of AuHgPdPt is only 38 mV, which is much smaller than that of AuHgPt (63 mV), AuHgPd (109 mV), and AuHgPtPd (78 mV), respectively. With the increase of voltage, the higher slope of the curve corresponds to the faster current density and the improved catalytic activity. As expected, the HER activities of Au and AuHg nanoparticles are negligible, while the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy is highly active. To investigate the intrinsic properties of the nanoalloys and the rate-determining step of the entire hydrogen evolution process, Tafel slopes were calculated from the HER polarization curves (Figure 4B). The Tafel slope of AuHgPdPt is 56.23 mV dec−1, which is smaller than AuHgPd (135.79 mV dec−1), AuHgPtPd (112.63 mV dec−1), AuHgPt (79.91 mV dec−1), and even the commercial Pt/C material (56.65 mV dec−1). The decrease in slope values indicates that the kinetics of HER is accelerated because the lower Tafel slope refers to the increase in the number of available active sites.65 In terms of the Tafel slope, it can be concluded that the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy has good HER electrocatalytic performance. The Tafel values and the EIS plots prove that the HER follows the Volmer−Tafel mechanism66

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H_{2}O+e^{-}=H_{ads}+OH^{-}}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{a d s}}=\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{O H}^{-}
\end{equation}

By extrapolating the Tafel plots, the exchange current density (j0) of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy was estimated to be 1.02 mA cm−2, exceeding those of AuHgPt (0.96 mA cm−2) and AuHgPtPd (0.84 mA cm−2) (Figure 4C). The stability of the HER is also a criterion for evaluating the performance of the catalyst. The chronoamperometry curve of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy at a current density of 10 mA cm−2 exhibited no substantial decay after 24 h, and the current density loss is less than 6% at 100 mA cm−2 after 24 h. After the electrochemical test, the morphology of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy is maintained, indicating the robust stability of the AuHgPdPt nanoalloy catalyst.

Based on the results of the electrochemical analysis, the possible reasons for the enhanced catalytic eﬀect of the quaternary AuHgPdPt hollow nanoalloy to the HER are speculated. In the two quaternary nanoalloys, metallic Pt and Pd occupy the main components for the electrochemical catalytic material. However, the Pd atoms after replacement in the AuHgPtPd nanoalloys are mainly oxidized states, indicating that exposed Pd is easy to oxidize in the environmental system. The Pd in oxidation state on the outside of the nanoalloy can aﬀect the electrocatalytic activity of the AuHgPtPd nanoalloy, which reduces the exchange current density compared with AuHgPt. The remarkable electrocatalytic activity of AuHgPdPt can be attributed to the synergistic eﬀect caused by the presence of metallic Pd. The theoretical basis for synergy is the “d-band center theory”. Based on this theory, the higher the position of the 3d center relative to the Fermi level, the stronger the interaction of the metal with the adsorbate.67 In the case of H adsorption, Pd has a higher radius and electronegativity than Pt, which causes the center of the d-band of Pt to move up; thus, its aﬃnity for H adsorption and cathode precipitation is further increased.68

