\begin{title}
3. CONCLUSIONS
\end{title}

In summary, a series of gold-based multiple noble metallic nanoalloys were successfully synthesized by the GRR using Hg as an intermediate. The structures, morphologies, composi- tions, and electrocatalytic activities of the nanoalloys were characterized. HAADF-STEM, EDS elemental maps, XRD, and XPS investigations found that the Pt and/or Pd-formed multimetallic alloys with the hollow Au nanoparticles were fabricated. In addition, by adjusting the order of substitution of Pt(IV) and Pd(II), gold-based hollow nanoalloys with diﬀerent electrochemical activities can be easily obtained under mild experimental conditions. Based on the electrochemical measurements, Pt-exposed nanoalloys exhibited excellent HER activities with low initial overpotential, small Tafel slope, and large exchange current density, which can be considered as a result of the synergistic eﬀect produced by the alloying of the metals since both metals are located at the apex of the volcanic map.

\begin{title}
4. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
\end{title}

4.1. Materials. Chloroauric acid (HAuCl4·4H2O), silver nitrate (AgNO3), sodium citrate, sodium borohydride (NaBH4), palladium- (II) chloride (PdCl2), potassium hydroxide (KOH), and chloropla- tinic acid (H2PtCl6) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai). Hydroxylamine hydrochloride (NH2OH·HCl) and mercury ion (Hg(II)) standard solution (100 mg/L) were obtained from Sigma-Aldrich. Deionized water (with a resistivity of 18.25 MΩ cm) was used in all experiments.

4.2. Synthesis of Hollow Au Nanoparticles. The formation of hollow Au nanoparticles is dependent on silver nanoparticles as sacriﬁcial templates.69 In the beginning, 29 mg of sodium citrate was completely dissolved in 100 mL of AgNO3 solution (0.2 mmol L−1) to obtain a homogeneous solution. Then, freshly prepared NaBH4 solution (2.0 mL, 0.1 mol L−1) in an ice−water bath was added to prepare silver nanoparticles. After stirring at 60 °C for 2 h and cooling to room temperature, 2.0 mL of 0.2 mol L−1 NH2OH·HCl solution was injected to 94 mL of the above solution, with the addition of 282 μL of AgNO3 (0.1 mol L−1) to minimize the synthesized silver nanoparticles. The solution was stirred at room temperature for 2 h to complete the reaction. Finally, 1.4 mL of HAuCl4 (25 mmol L−1) was added dropwise under magnetic stirring at 60 °C, with the color of the solution gradually evolving to blue. The reaction was stopped after 2 h of stirring. With the temperature cooling down to room temperature, the obtained hollow Au nanoparticles were collected by centrifuga- tion, washed, and redispersed in deionized water.

4.3. Synthesis of Hollow Nanoalloys. A total of 2 mL of hollow Au nanoparticle solution (1 OD), 100 μL of freshly prepared NaBH4 (0.01 mol L−1) aqueous solution, and 80.0 μL of Hg(II) standard solution (100 mg/L) were orderly added into a series of 10 mL beakers. After stirring for 30 min, the obtained amalgams (AuHg nanoparticles) were centrifuged. Subsequently, 1.2 mmol noble metal ions (PdCl2 or H2PtCl6) were added into the prepared AuHg amalgam solution (1 mL), and the solution was left undisturbed for 12 h. The products were centrifuged, washed thoroughly, and deﬁned as AuHgPt and AuHgPd nanoparticles. Similarly, the hollow Au nanoparticles were simply replaced by the ternary nanoalloys (AuHgPt and AuHgPd) to synthesize quaternary (AuHgPtPd or AuHgPdPt) hollow nanoalloys. The speciﬁc reaction equations for the GRRs are as follows: 2Hg + 2Cl−+ Pd2+ = Pd + Hg2Cl2, and [PtCl6]2−+ Hg = [PtCl4]2−+ Hg2+ + 2 Cl−, [PtCl4]2−+ 2 Hg + 2Cl− = Pt + Hg2Cl2 + 4Cl−.

4.4. Characterizations. The morphologies and microstructures of the obtained nanoalloys were characterized by a JEOL-JEM-1011-type transmission electron microscope with an acceleration voltage of 100 kV. The lattice spacing and misalignment images were obtained by HAADF-STEM, and elemental mapping images were acquired by EDS using a JEOL-2100F electron microscope equipped with a

STEM unit. The nanoalloys were examined with a Rigaku MiniFlex 600 (Japan) powder X-ray diﬀractometer with Cu Kα radiation (Kα1, λ1 = 1.5406 Å; Kα2, λ2 = 1.5444 Å), and the chemical states of the elements were measured by XPS (VG ESCALAB 220i-XL, equipped with a monochromatic Al X-ray source).

4.5. Electrochemical Measurement. The synthesized nano- alloys (2 mg mL−1, 20.0 μL) were dropped on glassy carbon electrodes. With the electrodes dried by an infrared lamp, 10 μL of Naﬁon solution (1 wt %) was cast and dried for use as working electrodes. All the electrochemical measurements, including LSV, EIS, and long-term stability tests, were performed using a CHI750E electrochemical workstation with a conventional three-electrode system. A graphite rod and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) were used as a counter electrode and a reference electrode, respectively. The EIS plots were reported in the form of Nyquist diagrams in a frequency range of 100 kHz to 1 mHz in a constant potential mode, and the applied overpotential was around −0.2 V for HER with an amplitude of 5 mV. All measurements were conducted at room temperature in 1.0 mol/L KOH aqueous solution. All potentials measured relative to the SCE electrode are calibrated to a RHE by eq 3, and the data are presented with an iR (i is the current and R is the resistance) correction.70

